{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876811798,"sessionID":"ses_1d2af8412ffeHKyDMfB20tm4O0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_VWHgbErat8twZxXKq7c1XICK","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r10s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\n+title: \"Split MEM read/write cones\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\n+  when the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\n+  the common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Split the MEM combinational work into distinct read and write subpaths so\n+  stores never exercise the load shift/extend network and loads never drag the\n+  store-data replication path through the same cone. Keep the architectural\n+  outputs identical, but let synthesis see smaller mutually exclusive logic so\n+  the hot memory stage routes and times better.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r10s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r10s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r10s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r10s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\n+title: 