_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved ...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Split MEM read/write cones\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the MEM combinational work into distinct read and write subpaths so\\nstores never exercise the load shift/extend network and loads never drag the\\nstore-data replication path through the same cone. Keep the architectural\\noutputs identical, but let synthesis see smaller mutually exclusive logic so\\nthe hot memory stage routes and times better.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.78, \"delta_pct\": -3.19, \"vs_baseline\": 16.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9818, \"ff\": 1960, \"seeds\": [150.99, 147.67, 133.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:33:43.049217Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"scribe_skipped\": \"PermissionError: scribe touched off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cor...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real IF/ID register so fetched instructions are latched once\\nbefore decode and hazard logic see them. That should shorten the\\nimem-to-decode-to-flush feedback cone and reduce the amount of logic that\\nsits on the fetch critical path, while preserving the architectural\\nretirement stream and existing stall/redirect rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retimes fetch through a registered IF/ID boundary so the PC update path no longer feeds decode combinationally in the same cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched fetch bundle as the decode input and keep the existing decode, operand read, and ID/EX semantics aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered IF/ID view for load-use checks so stall generation no longer sits on the same combinational fetch path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level stage boundary for the new fetch register while preserving the RVFI and memory I/O contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Short backedge delta predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny exact-match predictor for short taken backward B-type branches\\nonly. Train it from resolved EX redirects, but store a compact signed delta\\nrather than a full target so the fetch-side datapath stays narrow; if the\\nbranch offset does not fit the compressed form, fall back to the existing\\nEX-resolved path. Keep JAL/JALR and forward branches off the predictor fast\\npath so the hardware remains bounded and formal stays tractable.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the short-backedge lookup, compressed delta storage, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved branch metadata needed to train or invalidate the predictor on taken backedges and mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update path between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Drop regfile write-through\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The regfile currently pays for same-cycle write-first bypass on both read\\nports, even though the EX-stage forwarding network already resolves the real\\nRAW hazards. Removing that always-on compare/mux work should shorten the ID\\ncone a little and may help Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The regfile currently pays for same-cycle write-first bypass on both read\\nports, even though the EX-stage forwarding network already resolves the real\\nRAW hazards. Removing that always-on compare/mux work should shorten the ID\\ncone a little and may help Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the register file a plain synchronous-write, combinational-read block\\nwith no same-cycle write-through bypass on its read ports. The pipeline\\nalready forwards newer values in EX, so this should preserve architectural\\nbehavior while trimming redundant compare/mux logic from the hot decode path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the same-cycle write-first bypass from both read ports so the regfile read cone is simpler and the existing EX forwarding path handles freshness.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the same-cycle write-first bypass from both read ports so the regfile read cone is simpler and the existing EX forwarding path handles freshness.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.29, \"delta_pct\": -3.33, \"vs_baseline\": 16.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.45, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9749, \"ff\": 1957, \"seeds\": [153.8, 141.98, 147.45], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:55:54.017974Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11, \"scribe_sk...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and multiply on the fast one-cycle ALU path, but\\nmove only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that stalls the\\npipe when a divide is actually executing. This should shrink the common EX\\ncritical path without changing architectural results for the benchmark's\\nusual instruction mix.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that produces RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider is busy.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/rem...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"JAL fast-path predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict unconditional JALs in IF by decoding only the JAL opcode and\\ncomputing the J-type target on the fetch side. Carry the predicted next PC\\nalongside the instruction so EX can suppress the redundant redirect when the\\ntarget matches, leaving JALR and conditional branches on the existing path.\\nThis targets call traffic with far less state than the failed BTB/RAS style\\npredictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact prediction sideband fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so fetch can carry a predicted next PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize JAL in fetch, compute its target immediately, and mark the prediction as valid for the downstream stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the prediction sideband with the decoded instruction so EX can compare the predicted and resolved next PCs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the redundant redirect on correctly predicted JALs while keeping JALR and branch resolution unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file...","metadata":{"matches":33,"truncated":false},"title":"micro_opt|forward|hazard|stall|fmax|predictor","time":{"start":1778879069678,"end":1778879069727}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0e111c1d73c023d9016a078a5d8b788199a06fb402d62e1443"}},"id":"prt_e2d747de500131ZabL7zujXBBo","sessionID":"ses_1d28b920bffe6Yk5cv2V8sbKtD","messageID":"msg_e2d746f3a001qxjf9JGtDWY9KK"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879069769,"sessionID":"ses_1d28b920bffe6Yk5cv2V8sbKtD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_DbdstF2xZELRgfafKnJslxip","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving control-flow target math earlier only works if JALR still sees the freshest rs1 value; precomputing jump targets without airtight forwarding/stalling breaks formal on taken-jump semantics.\n2:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +18.70%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID can materially improve Fmax by cutting the EX-to-IF redirect cone, but the live JALR path still has to stay in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1 data.\n3:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.50%): A tiny static backward-branch predictor can still lose on a loop-heavy benchmark if the branch-immediate decode and legality checks extend the IF critical path more than the saved EX redirect flushes.\n4:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB can make formal blow past wall-clock limits, so fetch-side predictor state needs proof-time budgeting as well as performance tuning.\n5:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, -13.60%): Predecoding opcode/funct/register fields into the IF/ID bundle only helps if decode slicing was the dominant cost; on a small FPGA, widening the fetch-side combinational payload can cost more Fmax than the ID fanout it removes.\n6:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.16%): Carrying predecoded load/store lane metadata (aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign flag) from EX into MEM trims the memory-side combinational cone, but the gain is modest unless that path is actually the timing bottleneck.\n7:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -77.43%): Duplicating effective-address adders only pays off if the shared ALU cone is truly the limiter; on this memory-heavy CoreMark mix, carrying `eff_addr` through EX/MEM added plumbing and fanout without enough critical-path relief.\n8:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +0.00%): A second hot-target slot only helps if it beats the added fetch-side compare/mux cost; CoreMark’s alternating back-edges and call/return sites can still leave a tiny cache net-negative when it does not cut enough wrong-path flushes.\n9:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -22.68%): Reusing the ALU sum for JALR and hoisting `pc + 4` only helps if the execute cone is actually adder-bound; on this core, the saved 32-bit arithmetic was outweighed by the extra mux/fanout on the hot control-flow path.\n10:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (regression, -5.30%): Splitting RVFI fields into a separate retire snapshot only pays off if it actually removes enough width/fanout from the MEM/WB critical cone; if the new snapshot still rides the same retirement boundary, the extra plumbing can erase the timing win.\n11:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.99%): A tiny RAS only pays off if CoreMark’s return traffic is heavy enough to amortize the IF-side opcode/rd/rs1 classification and stack plumbing; otherwise the extra fetch-path work costs more than the JALR flushes it saves.\n12:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -5.21%): Splitting load extraction so `LW` bypasses the shift/extract logic only helps if that variable byte/halfword path is truly on the MEM critical cone; otherwise the added case plumbing and fanout can outweigh the saved shifter on a load-heavy CoreMark mix.\n13:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Moving conditional branch resolution into ID only works if that stage gets the same freshness guarantees as EX, because formal will immediately find stale-operand compares when branch forwarding or load-use stalls are incomplete.\n14:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use masks only help when their decode coverage is exact, because skipping a real rs2 dependency in forwarding or load-use logic turns into a register-state formal failure rather than an obvious stall bug.\n15:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (regression, -3.19%): Splitting MEM into separate read/write cones only helps when the removed load logic was truly sharing the critical path; on this core, the extra gating and control fanout cost more than the saved shift/extend work.\n16:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -21.68%): A short-loop exact-match backedge predictor still regresses if the training, tag compare, and delta bookkeeping on the fetch path cost more than the EX redirect flushes it saves.\n\n(End of file - total 16 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving control-flow target math earlier only works if JALR still sees the freshest rs1 value; precomputing jump targets without airtight forwarding/stalling breaks formal on taken-jump semantics.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +18.70%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID can materially improve Fmax by cutting the EX-to-IF redirect cone, but the live JALR path still has to stay in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1 data.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.50%): A tiny static backward-branch predictor can still lose on a loop-heavy benchmark if the branch-immediate decode and legality checks extend the IF critical path more than the saved EX redirect flushes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB can make formal blow past wall-clock limits, so fetch-side predictor state needs proof-time budgeting as well as performance tuning.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, -13.60%): Predecoding opcode/funct/register fields into the IF/ID bundle only helps if decode slicing was the dominant cost; on a small FPGA, widening the fetch-side combinational payload can cost more Fmax than the ID fanout it removes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.16%): Carrying predecoded load/store lane metadata (aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign flag) from EX into MEM trims the memory-side combinational cone, but the gain is modest unless that path is actually the timing bottleneck.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -77.43%): Duplicating effective-address adders only pays off if the shared ALU cone is truly the limiter; on this memory-heavy CoreMark mix, carrying `eff_addr` through EX/MEM added plumbing and fanout without enough critical-path relief.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +0.00%): A second hot-target slot only helps if it beats the added fetch-side compare/mux cost; CoreMark’s alternating back-edges and call/return sites can still leave a tiny cache net-negative when it does not cut enough wrong-path flushes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -22.68%): Reusing the ALU sum for JALR and hoisting `pc + 4` only helps if the execute cone is actually adder-bound; on this core, the saved 32-bit arithmetic was outweighed by the extra mux/fanout on the hot control-flow path.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (regression, -5.30%): Splitting RVFI fields into a separate retire snapshot only pays off if it actually removes enough width/fanout from the MEM/WB critical cone; if the new snapshot still rides the same retirement boundary, the extra plumbing can erase the timing win.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.99%): A tiny RAS only pays off if CoreMark’s return traffic is heavy enough to amortize the IF-side opcode/rd/rs1 classification and stack plumbing; otherwise the extra fetch-path work costs more than the JALR flushes it saves.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -5.21%): Splitting load extraction so `LW` bypasses the shift/extract logic only helps if that variable byte/halfword path is truly on the MEM critical cone; otherwise the added case plumbing and fanout can outweigh the saved shifter on a load-heavy CoreMark mix.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Moving conditional branch resolution into ID only works if that stage gets the same freshness guarantees as EX, because formal will immediately find stale-operand compares when branch forwarding or load-use stalls are incomplete.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use masks only help when their decode coverage is exact, because skipping a real rs2 dependency in forwarding or load-use logic turns into a register-state formal failure rather than an obvious stall bug.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (regression, -3.19%): Splitting MEM into separate read/write cones only helps when the removed load logic was truly sharing the critical path; on this core, the extra gating and control fanout cost more than the saved shift/extend work.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -21.68%): A short-loop exact-match backedge predictor still regresses if the training, tag compare, and delta bookkeeping on the fetch path cost more than the EX redirect flushes it saves.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778879069668,"end":1778879069768}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0e111c1d73c023d9016a078a5d8b648199814e090ba751e53f"}},"id":"prt_e2d747dd3001Y3DzMHPLltehly","sessionID":"ses_1d28b920bffe6Yk5cv2V8sbKtD","messageID":"msg_e2d746f3a001qxjf9JGtDWY9KK"}}
