{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778886294276,"sessionID":"ses_1d21e7bc3ffeNBDAzDp1N8UqTm","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_pO7YKfC0BBaYWHWYBDcarasq","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\n+title: \"Exact source-use gating\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The forwarding unit still evaluates both rs1 and rs2 compare trees every cycle, even when the current instruction can only read one source or none at all. On a small FPGA, that dead equality work is a plausible Fmax limiter on the hot execute path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add exact source-liveness bits to the decoded control bundle and use them to gate the forwarding compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot read. Keep the hazard/load-use logic unchanged so the fragile stall discipline stays intact, but let synthesis skip dead rs2 and other unused compare paths in the common case. This should trim a little fanout from the EX-side critical cone without changing architectural behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add exact source-liveness flags to the control bundle so later stages can tell which operands are actually read.\"\n+  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n+    description: \"Populate the source-liveness flags for each opcode class with exact coverage for RV32IM instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the new flags through the ID/EX register alongside the existing decoded control bundle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the ID/EX source-liveness flags into forwarding so the compare trees can be gated without touching the stall contract.\"\n+  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Skip equality comparisons for operands marked dead while preserving the existing EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r14s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\n+title: \"Exact source-use gating\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  The forwarding unit still evaluates both rs1 and rs2 compare trees every cycle, even when the current instruction can only read one source or none at all. On a small FPGA, that dead equality work is a plausible Fmax limiter on the hot execute path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add exact source-liveness bits to the decoded control bundle and use them to gate the forwarding compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot read. Keep the hazard/load-use logic unchanged so the fragile stall discipline stays intact, but let synthesis skip dead rs2 and other unused compare paths in the common case. This should trim a little fanout from the EX-side critical cone without changing architectural behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add exact source-liveness flags to the control bundle so later stages can tell which operands are actually read.\"\n+  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n+    description: \"Populate the source-liveness flags for each opcode class with exact coverage for RV32IM instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the new flags through the ID/EX register