{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876943404,"sessionID":"ses_1d2af8483ffe3SZy5iG74TFsrN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_QTe0uIegSPD6Y43JqyzylwaT","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r10s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\n+title: \"Retimed fetch boundary\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\n+  where `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\n+  the same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\n+  stage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\n+  target work already moved earlier.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Insert a real IF/ID register so fetched instructions are latched once\n+  before decode and hazard logic see them. That should shorten the\n+  imem-to-decode-to-flush feedback cone and reduce the amount of logic that\n+  sits on the fetch critical path, while preserving the architectural\n+  retirement stream and existing stall/redirect rules.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Retimes fetch through a registered IF/ID boundary so the PC update path no longer feeds decode combinationally in the same cycle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Consume the latched fetch bundle as the decode input and keep the existing decode, operand read, and ID/EX semantics aligned to the new boundary.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Use the registered IF/ID view for load-use checks so stall generation no longer sits on the same combinational fetch path.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Rewire the top-level stage boundary for the new fetch register while preserving the RVFI and memory I/O contract.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA c