{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778878005632,"sessionID":"ses_1d29c3be4ffea5larosxWK1oND","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_bbdFZTq3Z8gYGQPoDn2UYdGl","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r11s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\n+title: \"Split divider from ALU\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\n+  operations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\n+  even though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and multiply on the fast one-cycle ALU path, but\n+  move only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that stalls the\n+  pipe when a divide is actually executing. This should shrink the common EX\n+  critical path without changing architectural results for the benchmark's\n+  usual instruction mix.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n+    description: \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that produces RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider is busy.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r11s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini