{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879207039,"sessionID":"ses_1d28b9185ffeXmwKU9wUBHaVm1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_O1fMB6ji5FeaNyETYk3bkUzT","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r12s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\n+title: \"Add a fetch buffer\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\n+  hazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\n+  small FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\n+  A tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\n+  architectural retirement stream.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a one-entry fetch buffer that latches the fetched `pc`, `instr`, and\n+  valid bit before decode consumes them, while keeping the PC engine and\n+  redirect logic on a narrower path. This should decouple the fetch timing\n+  from the decode/hazard fanout, reduce the hot combinational span through the\n+  front end, and preserve the existing stall, flush, and RVFI behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Consume the buffered fetch bundle unchanged so decode and immediate generation stay aligned to the new boundary.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Rewire the top-level front end around the buffered fetch stage without changing the external I/O or RVFI contract.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r12s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/h