{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778886226554,"sessionID":"ses_1d21e7c28ffee2JvzTj4W7Ptvw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_hPiCdhng0A63M37dnTATKuuA","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving control-flow target math earlier only works if JALR still sees the freshest rs1 value; precomputing jump targets without airtight forwarding/stalling breaks formal on taken-jump semantics.\n2:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +18.70%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID can materially improve Fmax by cutting the EX-to-IF redirect cone, but the live JALR path still has to stay in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1 data.\n3:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.50%): A tiny static backward-branch predictor can still lose on a loop-heavy benchmark if the branch-immediate decode and legality checks extend the IF critical path more than the saved EX redirect flushes.\n4:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB can make formal blow past wall-clock limits, so fetch-side predictor state needs proof-time budgeting as well as performance tuning.\n5:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, -13.60%): Predecoding opcode/funct/register fields into the IF/ID bundle only helps if decode slicing was the dominant cost; on a small FPGA, widening the fetch-side combinational payload can cost more Fmax than the ID fanout it removes.\n6:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.16%): Carrying predecoded load/store lane metadata (aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign flag) from EX into MEM trims the memory-side combinational cone, but the gain is modest unless that path is actually the timing bottleneck.\n7:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -77.43%): Duplicating effective-address adders only pays off if the shared ALU cone is truly the limiter; on this memory-heavy CoreMark mix, carrying `eff_addr` through EX/MEM added plumbing and fanout without enough critical-path relief.\n8:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +0.00%): A second hot-target slot only helps if it beats the added fetch-side compare/mux cost; CoreMark’s alternating back-edges and call/return sites can still leave a tiny cache net-negative when it does not cut enough wrong-path flushes.\n9:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -22.68%): Reusing the ALU sum for JALR and hoisting `pc + 4` only helps if the execute cone is actually adder-bound; on this core, the saved 32-bit arithmetic was outweighed by the extra mux/fanout on the hot control-flow path.\n10:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (regression, -5.30%): Splitting RVFI fields into a separate retire snapshot only pays off if it actually removes enough width/fanout from the MEM/WB critical cone; if the new snapshot still rides the same retirement boundary, the extra plumbing can erase the timing win.\n11:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.99%): A tiny RAS only pays off if CoreMark’s return traffic is heavy enough to amortize the IF-side opcode/rd/rs1 classification and stack plumbing; otherwise the extra fetch-path work costs more than the JALR flushes it saves.\n12:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -5.21%): Splitting load extraction so `LW` bypasses the shift/extract logic only helps if that variable byte/halfword path is truly on the MEM critical cone; otherwise the added case plumbing and fanout can outweigh the saved shifter on a load-heavy CoreMark mix.\n13:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Moving conditional branch resolution into ID only works if that stage gets the same freshness guarantees as EX, because formal will immediately find stale-operand compares when branch forwarding or load-use stalls are incomplete.\n14:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use masks only help when their decode coverage is exact, because skipping a real rs2 dependency in forwarding or load-use logic turns into a register-state formal failure rather than an obvious stall bug.\n15:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (regression, -3.19%): Splitting MEM into separate read/write cones only helps when the removed load logic was truly sharing the critical path; on this core, the extra gating and control fanout cost more than the saved shift/extend work.\n16:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -21.68%): A short-loop exact-match backedge predictor still regresses if the training, tag compare, and delta bookkeeping on the fetch path cost more than the EX redirect flushes it saves.\n17:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r12s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use gating can shrink forwarding compares, but adding a committed architectural shadow just to recover RVFI rs?_rdata can bloat the formal state space enough to time out the proof.\n18:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r12s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry fetch buffer only helps if `pc`, `instr`, and `valid` are updated as one atomic bundle across stall/flush/redirect, otherwise the extra cycle can desynchronize control-flow state and break CoreMark cosim.\n19:    - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r12s2 (broken, n/a): A speculative canonical RAS must follow the same stall/flush/redirect contract as the rest of fetch, or `pc_fwd` will catch a mismatch between the predicted return target and the architectural next PC.\n\n(End of file - total 19 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving control-flow target math earlier only works if JALR still sees the freshest rs1 value; precomputing jump targets without airtight forwarding/stalling breaks formal on taken-jump semantics.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +18.70%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID can materially improve Fmax by cutting the EX-to-IF redirect cone, but the live JALR path still has to stay in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1 data.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.50%): A tiny static backward-branch predictor can still lose on a loop-heavy benchmark if the branch-immediate decode and legality checks extend the IF critical path more than the saved EX redirect flushes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB can make formal blow past wall-clock limits, so fetch-side predictor state needs proof-time budgeting as well as performance tuning.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, -13.60%): Predecoding opcode/funct/register fields into the IF/ID bundle only helps if decode slicing was the dominant cost; on a small FPGA, widening the fetch-side combinational payload can cost more Fmax than the ID fanout it removes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.16%): Carrying predecoded load/store lane metadata (aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign flag) from EX into MEM trims the memory-side combinational cone, but the gain is modest unless that path is actually the timing bottleneck.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -77.43%): Duplicating effective-address adders only pays off if the shared ALU cone is truly the limiter; on this memory-heavy CoreMark mix, carrying `eff_addr` through EX/MEM added plumbing and fanout without enough critical-path relief.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +0.00%): A second hot-target slot only helps if it beats the added fetch-side compare/mux cost; CoreMark’s alternating back-edges and call/return sites can still leave a tiny cache net-negative when it does not cut enough wrong-path flushes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -22.68%): Reusing the ALU sum for JALR and hoisting `pc + 4` only helps if the execute cone is actually adder-bound; on this core, the saved 32-bit arithmetic was outweighed by the extra mux/fanout on the hot control-flow path.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (regression, -5.30%): Splitting RVFI fields into a separate retire snapshot only pays off if it actually removes enough width/fanout from the MEM/WB critical cone; if the new snapshot still rides the same retirement boundary, the extra plumbing can erase the timing win.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.99%): A tiny RAS only pays off if CoreMark’s return traffic is heavy enough to amortize the IF-side opcode/rd/rs1 classification and stack plumbing; otherwise the extra fetch-path work costs more than the JALR flushes it saves.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -5.21%): Splitting load extraction so `LW` bypasses the shift/extract logic only helps if that variable byte/halfword path is truly on the MEM critical cone; otherwise the added case plumbing and fanout can outweigh the saved shifter on a load-heavy CoreMark mix.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Moving conditional branch resolution into ID only works if that stage gets the same freshness guarantees as EX, because formal will immediately find stale-operand compares when branch forwarding or load-use stalls are incomplete.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use masks only help when their decode coverage is exact, because skipping a real rs2 dependency in forwarding or load-use logic turns into a register-state formal failure rather than an obvious stall bug.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (regression, -3.19%): Splitting MEM into separate read/write cones only helps when the removed load logic was truly sharing the critical path; on this core, the extra gating and control fanout cost more than the saved shift/extend work.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -21.68%): A short-loop exact-match backedge predictor still regresses if the training, tag compare, and delta bookkeeping on the fetch path cost more than the EX redirect flushes it saves.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r12s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use gating can shrink forwarding compares, but adding a committed architectural shadow just to recover RVFI rs?_rdata can bloat the formal state space enough to time out the proof.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r12s1 (broken, n/a): A one-entry fetch buffer only helps if `pc`, `instr`, and `valid` are updated as one atomic bundle across stall/flush/redirect, otherwise the extra cycle can desynchronize control-flow state and break CoreMark cosim.\n   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r12s2 (broken, n/a): A speculative canonical RAS must follow the same stall/flush/redirect contract as the rest of fetch, or `pc_fwd` will catch a mismatch between the predicted return target and the architectural next PC.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778886226524,"end":1778886226551}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09a431e014cc5326016a07a6526344819a986e2749bfc359aa"}},"id":"prt_e2de1b243001cArdtvhuV1d8eW","sessionID":"ses_1d21e7c28ffee2JvzTj4W7Ptvw","messageID":"msg_e2de1847b001cMZiK1g1vQEKuK"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778886226572,"sessionID":"ses_1d21e7c28ffee2JvzTj4W7Ptvw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_THZ0MMUUQzwrYQt6BK922ic8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"structural|fetch buffer|atomic fetch latch|return stack|RAS|predecoded|branch resolution|retire snapshot","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","include":"*.jsonl"},"output":"Found 22 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Retime branch target generation\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes the taken-branch redirect path and feeds it\\nstraight back into the PC update logic in the same cycle. That creates a\\nwide EX-to-IF feedback cone that is a natural Fmax limiter on this small\\nin-order core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes the taken-branch redirect path and feeds it\\nstraight back into the PC update logic in the same cycle. That creates a\\nwide EX-to-IF feedback cone that is a natural Fmax limiter on this small\\nin-order core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Precompute the branch/JAL target in ID, carry it as a registered sideband\\nthrough the pipeline, and let EX use that latched target instead of\\nrecomputing pc+imm on the critical redirect path. This should shorten the\\nbranch feedback cone without changing branch penalties or architectural\\nbehavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a decoded branch-target sideband to the ID/EX bundle so it can be carried forward as a registered value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch pc+imm for branch/JAL instructions during decode, alongside the existing control signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched target for branch/JAL redirects and keep only the JALR adder on the live EX critical path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a decoded branch-target sideband to the ID/EX bundle so it can be carried forward as a registered value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch pc+imm for branch/JAL instructions during decode, alongside the existing control signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched target for branch/JAL redirects and keep...\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Backward branch taken predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops, and this baseline core still\\nfetches pc+4 until EX resolves every branch. That means each taken backward\\nloop branch pays a fixed wrong-path flush even though the direction is\\nobvious from the branch immediate sign bit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops, and this baseline core still\\nfetches pc+4 until EX resolves every branch. That means each taken backward\\nloop branch pays a fixed wrong-path flush even though the direction is\\nobvious from the branch immediate sign bit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny static predictor in IF that recognizes legal B-type branches and\\npredicts backward branches taken when the branch immediate is negative.\\nRecompute the same prediction in EX so correctly predicted branches do not\\nredirect, and only mispredictions flush the frontend. Keep JAL/JALR as EX-\\nresolved control flow to avoid introducing a BTB or return stack.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add static backward-branch prediction in fetch and choose the predicted next PC before EX resolves the branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare actual branch outcome against the IF prediction and redirect only on mispredict.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add static backward-branch prediction in fetch and choose the predicted next PC before EX resolves the branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare actual branch outcome against the IF prediction and redirect only on mispredict.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 306.86, \"delta_pct\": 8.5, \"vs_baseline\": 8.5, \"fmax_mhz\": 132.24, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4309463, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\"...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent did not write a file for slot hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (allowed_yaml_ids=['hyp-20260515-001-r2s0', 'hyp-20260515-002-r2s1', 'hyp-20260515-003-r2s2'])\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2}\n\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed operand bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage still pays for operand bypass selection on every\\ninstruction, even when the result is just a simple ALU op or a branch\\ncompare. Moving that muxing work earlier is a clean structural way to\\nshrink the common critical path without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage still pays for operand bypass selection on every\\ninstruction, even when the result is just a simple ALU op or a branch\\ncompare. Moving that muxing work earlier is a clean structural way to\\nshrink the common critical path without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move EX/MEM and MEM/WB bypass selection into ID, latch already-forwarded\\nrs1/rs2 values in the ID/EX register, and let EX consume stable operands\\ndirectly. Keep the existing load-use stall rule and x0 filtering intact so\\ncorrectness stays the same while the EX combinational cone gets shorter.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the bypass-source values into ID and remove the EX-stage forwarding plumbing from the top-level wiring.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve rs1/rs2 forwarding before the ID/EX flop and latch the selected operands into the pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume pre-forwarded operands directly and drop the operand-select muxes from the EX critical path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retime or simplify bypass selection so it serves the ID-side operand latch instead of EX.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the bypass-source values into ID and remove the EX-stage forwarding plumbing from the top-level wiring.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve rs1/rs2 forwardin...\n  Line 11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Retime load/store lane metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage computes byte masks, alignment, and load-data\\nextraction in one combinational block. CoreMark exercises a lot of simple\\nloads and stores, so shrinking that memory-side cone is a plausible way to\\nraise Fmax without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage computes byte masks, alignment, and load-data\\nextraction in one combinational block. CoreMark exercises a lot of simple\\nloads and stores, so shrinking that memory-side cone is a plausible way to\\nraise Fmax without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Precompute and carry the load/store lane metadata out of EX: word-aligned\\naddress, byte mask, and byte-lane shift/selection can be latched with the\\nEX/MEM payload so MEM only has to perform the actual memory access and a\\nmuch smaller extract/extend step. This should reduce the hot memory-path\\nlogic seen by synthesis while preserving retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add EX/MEM and MEM/WB sideband fields for predecoded load/store lane metadata and aligned address handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the lane metadata once the ALU result is known so the memory stage no longer needs to decode width/address details.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the carried metadata to simplify byte-mask, alignment, and load-extract logic on the MEM critical path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add EX/MEM and MEM/WB sideband fields for predecoded load/store lane metadata and aligned address handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the lane metadata once the ALU result is known so the memory stage no longer needs ...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Split divide from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU keeps the full RV32M divide/remainder logic in the common\\ncombinational datapath. That is expensive logic on a small FPGA, even though\\nCoreMark is much more branch- and multiply-heavy than divide-heavy. Removing\\nDIV/REM from the hot ALU cone should raise Fmax without changing the ISA-\\nvisible result stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU keeps the full RV32M divide/remainder logic in the common\\ncombinational datapath. That is expensive logic on a small FPGA, even though\\nCoreMark is much more branch- and multiply-heavy than divide-heavy. Removing\\nDIV/REM from the hot ALU cone should raise Fmax without changing the ISA-\\nvisible result stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the one-cycle ALU for ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and the multiply ops, but\\nmove DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that only stalls the\\npipe when a divide is actually executing. Expose the divide result back\\nthrough the existing EX/WB retirement path and preserve the RV32M corner\\ncases exactly. This is a pure structural split that should shrink the common\\ncritical path while leaving non-divide CoreMark code on the fast path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the divider/remainder combinational cone from the common ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that returns RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider r...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Predecode hot instruction fields\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current decode path fans the full 32-bit instruction into legality,\\nregister address extraction, and hazard checks. On a small FPGA, that\\nrepeated slicing and nested opcode logic is a plausible Fmax limiter once\\nthe branch-target cone has already been trimmed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current decode path fans the full 32-bit instruction into legality,\\nregister address extraction, and hazard checks. On a small FPGA, that\\nrepeated slicing and nested opcode logic is a plausible Fmax limiter once\\nthe branch-target cone has already been trimmed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move instruction-field extraction to the IF/ID boundary: latch opcode,\\nfunct3, funct7, rd, rs1, rs2, and EBREAK qualification in the pipeline\\nbundle, then have `decoder` and `hazard_unit` consume those predecoded\\nfields instead of re-slicing the raw instruction word. This should cut\\ndecode fanout and simplify the common ID critical path without changing\\nISA-visible behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predecoded IF/ID sideband fields for opcode/funct/register-address metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extract and latch the instruction metadata once when fetching, including EBREAK qualification.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrower predecoded fields instead of repeatedly slicing the raw instruction bus.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the latched source register addresses from IF/ID so load-use checks stop fanning out the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the new predecoded bundle through decode and keep the ID/EX register semantics unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg....\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Early branch resolution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage still carries branch compare, redirect selection, and the\\nforwarding muxes on the same path. CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight\\nloops, so taking conditional branches off that cone should help both Fmax\\nand the loop-throughput side of the fitness score.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage still carries branch compare, redirect selection, and the\\nforwarding muxes on the same path. CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight\\nloops, so taking conditional branches off that cone should help both Fmax\\nand the loop-throughput side of the fitness score.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move B-type branch resolution into ID so BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU can\\nredirect one stage earlier, while leaving JALR in EX to preserve the\\nfreshness guarantees on rs1. The common case then stops paying branch\\ncompare cost in EX, and the hot redirect path becomes shorter without\\nchanging architectural results.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can see fresher rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve conditional branch compare and misalignment checks in decode, then emit the early redirect information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove branch compare work from the hot EX cone and keep JALR plus non-branch ALU work on the live execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire redirect control so the ID-stage branch resolver can drive fetch without waiting for EX on B-type branches.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can see fresher rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"descri...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Retimed memory lane metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still recomputes byte masks, alignment, and load extraction\\nfrom the full ALU result on every load/store. On a small FPGA, that keeps\\na fairly wide combinational cone on the memory side of the pipe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still recomputes byte masks, alignment, and load extraction\\nfrom the full ALU result on every load/store. On a small FPGA, that keeps\\na fairly wide combinational cone on the memory side of the pipe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Precompute the load/store lane metadata in EX and carry it through the\\nEX/MEM payload so MEM only performs the actual bus access plus a much\\nsmaller extract/extend step. This should trim the common memory-path logic\\nwithout changing retirement semantics or ISA-visible behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add EX/MEM sideband fields for predecoded lane metadata such as alignment, mask, and shift information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the memory lane metadata once the effective address is known, instead of re-deriving it in MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the carried metadata so byte-mask, alignment, and load-extract logic no longer dominate the MEM cone.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add EX/MEM sideband fields for predecoded lane metadata such as alignment, mask, and shift information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the memory lane metadata once the effective address is known, instead of re-deriving it in MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the carried metadata so byte-mask, alignment, and load-extract logic no longer dominate the MEM cone.\"}], \"outcome\": \"i...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Dedicated effective-address fast path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Loads, stores, and JALR all currently route base-plus-offset address formation through the same EX datapath that also serves arithmetic and branch work. On a small FPGA, that shared cone is a natural Fmax limiter, especially in a CoreMark mix with frequent memory traffic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Loads, stores, and JALR all currently route base-plus-offset address formation through the same EX datapath that also serves arithmetic and branch work. On a small FPGA, that shared cone is a natural Fmax limiter, especially in a CoreMark mix with frequent memory traffic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated effective-address adder in EX for LOAD, STORE, and JALR, and carry its result as a separate EX/MEM sideband so the main ALU only handles true arithmetic and logic ops. MEM then consumes the sideband directly for address, alignment, and RVFI memory fields. This should shorten the hot execute path without changing architectural retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM sideband field for the dedicated effective-address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute LOAD/STORE/JALR addresses with a separate adder instead of routing them through the main ALU cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the carried effective-address sideband for memory access and RVFI memory reporting.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM sideband field for the dedicated effective-address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute LOAD/STORE/JALR addresses with a separate adder instead of routing them through the main ALU cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the ...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Split retire snapshot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB bundle carries both writeback state and a large set of\\nRVFI-only retire fields through the hottest part of the pipeline. That wide\\npayload increases routing and fanout even though most of it is only needed at\\ncommit time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB bundle carries both writeback state and a large set of\\nRVFI-only retire fields through the hottest part of the pipeline. That wide\\npayload increases routing and fanout even though most of it is only needed at\\ncommit time.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the retirement metadata from the live writeback path: keep a narrow\\nMEM/WB register for regfile writeback and forwarding, and capture the RVFI\\nfields into a separate commit snapshot at the retirement boundary. This should\\nreduce the width and fanout of the common MEM/WB/WB cone while preserving the\\narchitectural stream and all RVFI semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define a slimmer live writeback bundle plus a separate retire-snapshot type for RVFI-only metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the narrow writeback state and a separate retirement snapshot instead of carrying one wide shared bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume only the narrow writeback bundle for regfile updates so the fast path no longer drags RVFI fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the retire snapshot to the RVFI outputs while keeping the execution/writeback pipeline semantics unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define a slimmer live writeback bundle plus a separate retire-snapshot type for RVFI-only metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the narr...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark and the runtime library spend real time in call/return traffic,\\nbut this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch. A bounded\\nreturn predictor should cut those recurring wrong-path flushes without\\npulling branch-immediate decode or a general BTB into the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark and the runtime library spend real time in call/return traffic,\\nbut this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch. A bounded\\nreturn predictor should cut those recurring wrong-path flushes without\\npulling branch-immediate decode or a general BTB into the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry return-address stack that predicts canonical link-\\nregister returns from the fetched opcode/rd/rs1 pattern, and train it only\\nfrom resolved JAL/JALR control flow in EX. Keep B-type branches and non-\\nreturn JALRs on the existing EX-resolved path, so the predictor stays small,\\nformal-friendly, and focused on the call/return behavior CoreMark actually\\nexercises.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 2-entry return-address stack in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate stack push/pop update information from resolved JAL and JALR control flow, and redirect on return mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 2-entry return-address stack in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Gen...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"ID branch resolve\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops, so conditional branch\\nredirects are on a hot path even in a simple in-order core. Moving the\\nB-type compare and redirect decision earlier should shorten the common\\nexecute cone and reduce the cost of taken loop branches.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops, so conditional branch\\nredirects are on a hot path even in a simple in-order core. Moving the\\nB-type compare and redirect decision earlier should shorten the common\\nexecute cone and reduce the cost of taken loop branches.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve B-type branches in ID using the already-decoded branch target and\\nforwarded source operands, while leaving JALR and all non-branch ALU work\\nin EX. This should remove branch compare and redirect selection from the\\nhot EX path without changing architectural results or the load-use stall\\ndiscipline.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact branch-resolve sideband so the ID-stage decision can flow through the pipeline cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can obtain fresh rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare B-type branches in decode, latch the taken decision and redirect target, and emit the early redirect info.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove conditional branch compare work from EX and keep JALR plus ordinary ALU work on the live execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Merge the ID-stage branch redirect with the existing control-flow path while preserving the RVFI and memory contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Split RV32M from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU computes RV32M products and quotients inside the same\\ncombinational block as ADD/SUB/logic/shift work. That makes the execute\\npath wider than it needs to be, even on instructions that never use the\\nM-extension.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU computes RV32M products and quotients inside the same\\ncombinational block as ADD/SUB/logic/shift work. That makes the execute\\npath wider than it needs to be, even on instructions that never use the\\nM-extension.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a dedicated RV32M side\\nunit so the common integer ALU only carries RV32I operations. Keep the\\nmultiply path single-cycle if the FPGA maps it well, and let DIV/REM use a\\nsmall stalled engine; this should shrink the hot EX cone without changing\\narchitectural results.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove RV32M execution from the common ALU datapath and keep the module focused on RV32I arithmetic and logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route M-extension ops into the dedicated side unit and preserve the existing retirement/writeback behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat a busy divide path as a stall source so the pipe only waits when the side unit is actually occupied.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the dedicated RV32M execution unit with correct multiply and divide/remainder corner cases.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove RV32M execution from the common ALU datapath and keep the module focused on RV32I arithmetic and logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route M-extension ops into the dedicated side unit and preserve the existing retirem...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry backedge cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry exact-match backedge cache in IF and train it only from resolved taken backward B-type branches. Use the cached target on a PC hit, ignore JAL/JALR and forward branches, and keep replacement to a simple bounded policy so the hardware stays small and the fetch cone stays short. This should recover repeated loop backedges with less logic than the earlier general-purpose predictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved ...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real IF/ID register so fetched instructions are latched once\\nbefore decode and hazard logic see them. That should shorten the\\nimem-to-decode-to-flush feedback cone and reduce the amount of logic that\\nsits on the fetch critical path, while preserving the architectural\\nretirement stream and existing stall/redirect rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retimes fetch through a registered IF/ID boundary so the PC update path no longer feeds decode combinationally in the same cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched fetch bundle as the decode input and keep the existing decode, operand read, and ID/EX semantics aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered IF/ID view for load-use checks so stall generation no longer sits on the same combinational fetch path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level stage boundary for the new fetch register while preserving the RVFI and memory I/O contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Short backedge delta predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny exact-match predictor for short taken backward B-type branches\\nonly. Train it from resolved EX redirects, but store a compact signed delta\\nrather than a full target so the fetch-side datapath stays narrow; if the\\nbranch offset does not fit the compressed form, fall back to the existing\\nEX-resolved path. Keep JAL/JALR and forward branches off the predictor fast\\npath so the hardware remains bounded and formal stays tractable.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the short-backedge lookup, compressed delta storage, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved branch metadata needed to train or invalidate the predictor on taken backedges and mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update path between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and multiply on the fast one-cycle ALU path, but\\nmove only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that stalls the\\npipe when a divide is actually executing. This should shrink the common EX\\ncritical path without changing architectural results for the benchmark's\\nusual instruction mix.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that produces RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider is busy.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/rem...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"JAL fast-path predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict unconditional JALs in IF by decoding only the JAL opcode and\\ncomputing the J-type target on the fetch side. Carry the predicted next PC\\nalongside the instruction so EX can suppress the redundant redirect when the\\ntarget matches, leaving JALR and conditional branches on the existing path.\\nThis targets call traffic with far less state than the failed BTB/RAS style\\npredictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact prediction sideband fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so fetch can carry a predicted next PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize JAL in fetch, compute its target immediately, and mark the prediction as valid for the downstream stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the prediction sideband with the decoded instruction so EX can compare the predicted and resolved next PCs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the redundant redirect on correctly predicted JALs while keeping JALR and branch resolution unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Add a fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\\nhazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\\nsmall FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\\nA tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\\narchitectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\\nhazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\\nsmall FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\\nA tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\\narchitectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch buffer that latches the fetched `pc`, `instr`, and\\nvalid bit before decode consumes them, while keeping the PC engine and\\nredirect logic on a narrower path. This should decouple the fetch timing\\nfrom the decode/hazard fanout, reduce the hot combinational span through the\\nfront end, and preserve the existing stall, flush, and RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the buffered fetch bundle unchanged so decode and immediate generation stay aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level front end around the buffered fetch stage without changing the external I/O or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", ...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Canonical return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic, but the core only\\nfast-paths unconditional JAL today. A tiny return predictor can remove the\\nrecurring JALR redirections from library calls without pulling a general BTB\\nor wider branch history into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic, but the core only\\nfast-paths unconditional JAL today. A tiny return predictor can remove the\\nrecurring JALR redirections from library calls without pulling a general BTB\\nor wider branch history into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry return-address stack that trains only on canonical calls\\nusing x1/x5 link registers and predicts only canonical return JALR patterns\\n(rs1 = x1/x5, imm = 0, rd = x0). Keep conditional branches and non-return\\nJALR on the existing EX-resolved path, and reuse the existing predicted-next-\\nPC sideband so IF can select the return target without adding a general target\\ncache. This should trim CoreMark's call/return flushes while keeping the\\npredictor state tiny and bounded.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small return-stack lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate return-stack push/pop update signals from resolved JAL and JALR control flow, and redirect on return mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small return-stack lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like...\n  Line 39: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Atomic fetch latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The front end still exposes a wide combinational `pc`/`instr`/`valid`\\nbundle to decode and hazard logic in the same cycle that fetch updates\\nthe PC. On this small FPGA, that fetch-to-decode feedback cone is one of\\nthe few remaining structural paths left to retime after the control-flow\\npredictor work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The front end still exposes a wide combinational `pc`/`instr`/`valid`\\nbundle to decode and hazard logic in the same cycle that fetch updates\\nthe PC. On this small FPGA, that fetch-to-decode feedback cone is one of\\nthe few remaining structural paths left to retime after the control-flow\\npredictor work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the purely combinational fetch-facing bundle with a real atomic\\nIF/ID latch that updates `pc`, `instr`, `valid`, and the prediction sideband\\ntogether on stall, flush, and redirect. Keep decode and hazard checks\\nconsuming only the latched bundle so no stage still depends on raw\\n`imem_data` in the same cycle. This should shorten the front-end critical\\npath without changing the architectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn fetch into the sole owner of an atomic IF/ID latch so `pc`, `instr`, `valid`, and prediction sideband fields update together on stall/flush/redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the registered fetch bundle unchanged and avoid any new dependence on raw `imem_data` in decode.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep load-use detection and stall generation on the latched instruction bundle rather than the live fetch cone.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn fetch into the sole owner of an atomic IF/ID latch so `pc`, `instr`, `v...","metadata":{"matches":22,"truncated":false},"title":"structural|fetch buffer|atomic fetch latch|return stack|RAS|predecoded|branch resolution|retire snapshot","time":{"start":1778886226548,"end":1778886226571}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09a431e014cc5326016a07a6526354819a9e6c2f5938c0a62d"}},"id":"prt_e2de1b2600018OVFRXkvBm5Rha","sessionID":"ses_1d21e7c28ffee2JvzTj4W7Ptvw","messageID":"msg_e2de1847b001cMZiK1g1vQEKuK"}}
