{"type":"text","timestamp":1778889286168,"sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a54ffeDPpM1OmIIRz022","part":{"id":"prt_e2e106188001IQgC9l3cD5jNrj","messageID":"msg_e2e0fac150010p2nxs0PmNj7tt","sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a54ffeDPpM1OmIIRz022","type":"text","text":"I’ve confirmed the prior structural work mostly hit front-end retiming, predictor, and retire-payload ideas. I’m going with a fetch-side structural retime that is distinct from the failed one-entry buffer: a small atomic skid buffer/queue in front of decode.","time":{"start":1778889286024,"end":1778889286041},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_09d1070a72400ebc016a07b2459474819aa1663220e5e3c8c2","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778889286258,"sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a54ffeDPpM1OmIIRz022","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"glob","callID":"call_xLRsJVKcPnPeKQ3EtsNkCYuK","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"*.yaml","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses"},"output":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r15s0.yaml","metadata":{"count":1,"truncated":false},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses","time":{"start":1778889286150,"end":1778889286256}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09d1070a72400ebc016a07b245d728819a8f744c434382eed0"}},"id":"prt_e2e10619a0010U9PhgUTAOVt0W","sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a54ffeDPpM1OmIIRz022","messageID":"msg_e2e0fac150010p2nxs0PmNj7tt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778889286302,"sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a54ffeDPpM1OmIIRz022","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_iV8VhwFPiXNAjPlU700C5PH9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","offset":28,"limit":20},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry backedge cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry exact-match backedge cache in IF and train it only from resolved taken backward B-type branches. Use the cached target on a PC hit, ignore JAL/JALR and forward branches, and keep replacement to a simple bounded policy so the hardware stays small and the fetch cone stays short. This should recover repeated loop backedges with less logic than the earlier general-purpose predictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Split MEM read/write cones\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the MEM combinational work into distinct read and write subpaths so\\nstores never exercise the load shift/extend network and loads never drag the\\nstore-data replication path through the same cone. Keep the architectural\\noutputs identical, but let synthesis see smaller mutually exclusive logic so\\nthe hot memory stage routes and times better.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.78, \"delta_pct\": -3.19, \"vs_baseline\": 16.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9818, \"ff\": 1960, \"seeds\": [150.99, 147.67, 133.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:33:43.049217Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"scribe_skipped\": \"PermissionError: scribe touched off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cor... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real IF/ID register so fetched instructions are latched once\\nbefore decode and hazard logic see them. That should shorten the\\nimem-to-decode-to-flush feedback cone and reduce the amount of logic that\\nsits on the fetch critical path, while preserving the architectural\\nretirement stream and existing stall/redirect rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retimes fetch through a registered IF/ID boundary so the PC update path no longer feeds decode combinationally in the same cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched fetch bundle as the decode input and keep the existing decode, operand read, and ID/EX semantics aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered IF/ID view for load-use checks so stall generation no longer sits on the same combinational fetch path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level stage boundary for the new fetch register while preserving the RVFI and memory I/O contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Short backedge delta predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny exact-match predictor for short taken backward B-type branches\\nonly. Train it from resolved EX redirects, but store a compact signed delta\\nrather than a full target so the fetch-side datapath stays narrow; if the\\nbranch offset does not fit the compressed form, fall back to the existing\\nEX-resolved path. Keep JAL/JALR and forward branches off the predictor fast\\npath so the hardware remains bounded and formal stays tractable.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the short-backedge lookup, compressed delta storage, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved branch metadata needed to train or invalidate the predictor on taken backedges and mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update path between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Drop regfile write-through\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The regfile currently pays for same-cycle write-first bypass on both read\\nports, even though the EX-stage forwarding network already resolves the real\\nRAW hazards. Removing that always-on compare/mux work should shorten the ID\\ncone a little and may help Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The regfile currently pays for same-cycle write-first bypass on both read\\nports, even though the EX-stage forwarding network already resolves the real\\nRAW hazards. Removing that always-on compare/mux work should shorten the ID\\ncone a little and may help Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the register file a plain synchronous-write, combinational-read block\\nwith no same-cycle write-through bypass on its read ports. The pipeline\\nalready forwards newer values in EX, so this should preserve architectural\\nbehavior while trimming redundant compare/mux logic from the hot decode path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the same-cycle write-first bypass from both read ports so the regfile read cone is simpler and the existing EX forwarding path handles freshness.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the same-cycle write-first bypass from both read ports so the regfile read cone is simpler and the existing EX forwarding path handles freshness.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.29, \"delta_pct\": -3.33, \"vs_baseline\": 16.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.45, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9749, \"ff\": 1957, \"seeds\": [153.8, 141.98, 147.45], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:55:54.017974Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11, \"scribe_sk... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and multiply on the fast one-cycle ALU path, but\\nmove only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side