nfidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the single-entry return predictor lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the return predictor from resolved canonical calls/returns and suppress redundant redirects on correct predictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the single-... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(End of file - total 43 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry backedge cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry exact-match backedge cache in IF and train it only from resolved taken backward B-type branches. Use the cached target on a PC hit, ignore JAL/JALR and forward branches, and keep replacement to a simple bounded policy so the hardware stays small and the fetch cone stays short. This should recover repeated loop backedges with less logic than the earlier general-purpose predictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Split MEM read/write cones\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the MEM combinational work into distinct read and write subpaths so\\nstores never exercise the load shift/extend network and loads never drag the\\nstore-data replication path through the same cone. Keep the architectural\\noutputs identical, but let synthesis see smaller mutually exclusive logic so\\nthe hot memory stage routes and times better.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.78, \"delta_pct\": -3.19, \"vs_baseline\": 16.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9818, \"ff\": 1960, \"seeds\": [150.99, 147.67, 133.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:33:43.049217Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"scribe_skipped\": \"PermissionError: scribe touched off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cor... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real IF/ID register so fetched instructions are latched once\\nbefore decode and hazard logic see them. That should shorten the\\nimem-to-decode-to-flush feedback cone and reduce the amount of logic that\\nsits on the fetch critical path, while preserving the architectural\\nretirement stream and existing stall/redirect rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retimes fetch through a registered IF/ID boundary so the PC update path no longer feeds decode combinationally in the same cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched fetch bundle as the decode input and keep the existing decode, operand read, and ID/EX semantics aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered IF/ID view for load-use checks so stall generation no longer sits on the same combinational fetch path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level stage boundary for the new fetch register while preserving the RVFI and memory I/O contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Short backedge delta predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny exact-match predictor for short taken backward B-type branches\\nonly. Train it from resolved EX redirects, but store a compact signed delta\\nrather than a full target so the fetch-side datapath stays narrow; if the\\nbranch offset does not fit the compressed form, fall back to the existing\\nEX-resolved path. Keep JAL/JALR and forward branches off the predictor fast\\npath so the hardware remains bounded and formal stays tractable.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the short-backedge lookup, compressed delta storage, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved branch metadata needed to train or invalidate the predictor on taken backedges and mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update path between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Drop regfile write-through\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The regfile currently pays for same-cycle write-first bypass on both read\\nports, even though the EX-stage forwarding network already resolves the real\\nRAW hazards. Removing that always-on compare/mux work should shorten the ID\\ncone a little and may help Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The regfile currently pays for same-cycle write-first bypass on both read\\nports, even though the EX-stage forwarding network already resolves the real\\nRAW hazards. Removing that always-on compare/mux work should shorten the ID\\ncone a little and may help Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the register file a plain synchronous-write, combinational-read block\\nwith no same-cycle write-through bypass on its read ports. The pipeline\\nalready forwards newer values in EX, so this should preserve architectural\\nbehavior while trimming redundant compare/mux logic from the hot decode path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the same-cycle write-first bypass from both read ports so the regfile read cone is simpler and the existing EX forwarding path handles freshness.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the same-cycle write-first bypass from both read ports so the regfile read cone is simpler and the existing EX forwarding path handles freshness.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.29, \"delta_pct\": -3.33, \"vs_baseline\": 16.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.45, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9749, \"ff\": 1957, \"seeds\": [153.8, 141.98, 147.45], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:55:54.017974Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11, \"scribe_sk... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"DIV/REM still sit in the same combinational ALU block as the common RV32I\\noperations. On a small FPGA, that divider cone is a likely Fmax limiter\\neven though CoreMark should spend most of its time on non-divide work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and multiply on the fast one-cycle ALU path, but\\nmove only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that stalls the\\npipe when a divide is actually executing. This should shrink the common EX\\ncritical path without changing architectural results for the benchmark's\\nusual instruction mix.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that produces RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider is busy.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove divide/remainder execution from the common combinational ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/rem... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"JAL fast-path predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict unconditional JALs in IF by decoding only the JAL opcode and\\ncomputing the J-type target on the fetch side. Carry the predicted next PC\\nalongside the instruction so EX can suppress the redundant redirect when the\\ntarget matches, leaving JALR and conditional branches on the existing path.\\nThis targets call traffic with far less state than the failed BTB/RAS style\\npredictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact prediction sideband fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so fetch can carry a predicted next PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize JAL in fetch, compute its target immediately, and mark the prediction as valid for the downstream stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the prediction sideband with the decoded instruction so EX can compare the predicted and resolved next PCs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the redundant redirect on correctly predicted JALs while keeping JALR and branch resolution unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Live-source compare gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends much of its time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource operands, yet the forwarding and load-use logic still evaluates the\\nfull rs1/rs2 compare trees every cycle. On a small FPGA, that dead equality\\nwork is a plausible Fmax limiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends much of its time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource operands, yet the forwarding and load-use logic still evaluates the\\nfull rs1/rs2 compare trees every cycle. On a small FPGA, that dead equality\\nwork is a plausible Fmax limiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add compact source-use flags to the decoded control bundle and use them to\\ngate only the compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot\\nread. Instructions like OP-IMM, loads, JAL, LUI, and AUIPC should skip the\\ndead rs2 path entirely while preserving the existing forwarding priority\\nand load-use stall rules. This should trim the common ID/EX cone without\\nchanging architectural behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact source-use flags to the control bundle so later stages can tell whether rs1 and rs2 are live.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the source-use flags for each opcode class while keeping the existing legality and control decode unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the new source-use flags into the ID/EX register alongside the existing decoded control bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate the rs1/rs2 equality trees with the live-source flags so dead operands do not pay for compare work.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the same live-source flags to skip unnecessary load-use comparisons for operands the instruction cannot actually read.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Add a fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\\nhazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\\nsmall FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\\nA tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\\narchitectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\\nhazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\\nsmall FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\\nA tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\\narchitectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch buffer that latches the fetched `pc`, `instr`, and\\nvalid bit before decode consumes them, while keeping the PC engine and\\nredirect logic on a narrower path. This should decouple the fetch timing\\nfrom the decode/hazard fanout, reduce the hot combinational span through the\\nfront end, and preserve the existing stall, flush, and RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the buffered fetch bundle unchanged so decode and immediate generation stay aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level front end around the buffered fetch stage without changing the external I/O or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Canonical return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic, but the core only\\nfast-paths unconditional JAL today. A tiny return predictor can remove the\\nrecurring JALR redirections from library calls without pulling a general BTB\\nor wider branch history into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic, but the core only\\nfast-paths unconditional JAL today. A tiny return predictor can remove the\\nrecurring JALR redirections from library calls without pulling a general BTB\\nor wider branch history into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry return-address stack that trains only on canonical calls\\nusing x1/x5 link registers and predicts only canonical return JALR patterns\\n(rs1 = x1/x5, imm = 0, rd = x0). Keep conditional branches and non-return\\nJALR on the existing EX-resolved path, and reuse the existing predicted-next-\\nPC sideband so IF can select the return target without adding a general target\\ncache. This should trim CoreMark's call/return flushes while keeping the\\npredictor state tiny and bounded.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small return-stack lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate return-stack push/pop update signals from resolved JAL and JALR control flow, and redirect on return mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small return-stack lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Gate always-on multiplier work\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends most cycles on non-M instructions, but the current ALU still\\ncomputes three 64-bit products on every cycle before the opcode case selects\\na result. That keeps multiplier fanout in the common execute cone even when\\nthe instruction is just ADD, LOAD, BRANCH, or a shift.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends most cycles on non-M instructions, but the current ALU still\\ncomputes three 64-bit products on every cycle before the opcode case selects\\na result. That keeps multiplier fanout in the common execute cone even when\\nthe instruction is just ADD, LOAD, BRANCH, or a shift.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the 64-bit product generation behind the M-extension arm so the common\\nnon-M path does not pay for always-on multiply logic. Keep the ALTOPS branch\\nand the real RV32M branch behaviorally identical, but let synthesis see a\\nsmaller fast path for the instructions CoreMark executes most often.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the signed and unsigned 64-bit products only when an M-extension opcode is selected, while preserving the existing ALTOPS and real-arithmetic results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the signed and unsigned 64-bit products only when an M-extension opcode is selected, while preserving the existing ALTOPS and real-arithmetic results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 314.8, \"delta_pct\": -7.92, \"vs_baseline\": 11.31, \"fmax_mhz\": 139.84, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4442217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10196, \"ff\": 1991, \"seeds\": [131.79, 139.84, 142.82], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T22:51:28.803371Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 13,... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Atomic fetch latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The front end still exposes a wide combinational `pc`/`instr`/`valid`\\nbundle to decode and hazard logic in the same cycle that fetch updates\\nthe PC. On this small FPGA, that fetch-to-decode feedback cone is one of\\nthe few remaining structural paths left to retime after the control-flow\\npredictor work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The front end still exposes a wide combinational `pc`/`instr`/`valid`\\nbundle to decode and hazard logic in the same cycle that fetch updates\\nthe PC. On this small FPGA, that fetch-to-decode feedback cone is one of\\nthe few remaining structural paths left to retime after the control-flow\\npredictor work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the purely combinational fetch-facing bundle with a real atomic\\nIF/ID latch that updates `pc`, `instr`, `valid`, and the prediction sideband\\ntogether on stall, flush, and redirect. Keep decode and hazard checks\\nconsuming only the latched bundle so no stage still depends on raw\\n`imem_data` in the same cycle. This should shorten the front-end critical\\npath without changing the architectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn fetch into the sole owner of an atomic IF/ID latch so `pc`, `instr`, `valid`, and prediction sideband fields update together on stall/flush/redirect.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the registered fetch bundle unchanged and avoid any new dependence on raw `imem_data` in decode.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep load-use detection and stall generation on the latched instruction bundle rather than the live fetch cone.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Turn fetch into the sole owner of an atomic IF/ID latch so `pc`, `instr`, `v... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Canonical call/return fast-path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The JAL fast-path already showed that cheap control-flow speculation can\\nimprove this core. CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic,\\nso a bounded return predictor is the next reusable control-flow pattern to\\nattack without introducing a general BTB.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The JAL fast-path already showed that cheap control-flow speculation can\\nimprove this core. CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic,\\nso a bounded return predictor is the next reusable control-flow pattern to\\nattack without introducing a general BTB.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current JAL fetch-side prediction, and add a tiny canonical\\nreturn predictor that only tracks x1/x5 link-register returns. IF should\\nuse the remembered return target immediately for matching JALR fetches,\\nwhile EX remains the source of truth for training and mispredict recovery.\\nThis should recover recurring call/return flushes more cheaply than a\\ngeneral target cache and stay small enough for Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the canonical return lookup in fetch and feed its predicted next PC into the existing control-flow path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the return predictor from resolved JAL/JALR control flow and redirect on mispredicts only.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the canonical return lookup in fetch and feed its predicted next PC into the existing control-flow path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the return predictor from resolved JAL/JALR control fl... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Exact source-use gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The forwarding unit still evaluates both rs1 and rs2 compare trees every cycle, even when the current instruction can only read one source or none at all. On a small FPGA, that dead equality work is a plausible Fmax limiter on the hot execute path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The forwarding unit still evaluates both rs1 and rs2 compare trees every cycle, even when the current instruction can only read one source or none at all. On a small FPGA, that dead equality work is a plausible Fmax limiter on the hot execute path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact source-liveness bits to the decoded control bundle and use them to gate the forwarding compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot read. Keep the hazard/load-use logic unchanged so the fragile stall discipline stays intact, but let synthesis skip dead rs2 and other unused compare paths in the common case. This should trim a little fanout from the EX-side critical cone without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add exact source-liveness flags to the control bundle so later stages can tell which operands are actually read.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the source-liveness flags for each opcode class with exact coverage for RV32IM instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the new flags through the ID/EX register alongside the existing decoded control bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ID/EX source-liveness flags into forwarding so the compare trees can be gated without touching the stall contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Skip equality comparisons for operands marked dead while preserving the existing EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"ID-side bypass latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"EX still pays for bypass selection on every instruction, even when the\\noperation is a simple ALU op or a branch compare. Retiming that mux work\\ninto ID should shrink the common execute cone while leaving the ISA-visible\\nretirement stream unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"EX still pays for bypass selection on every instruction, even when the\\noperation is a simple ALU op or a branch compare. Retiming that mux work\\ninto ID should shrink the common execute cone while leaving the ISA-visible\\nretirement stream unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding in ID, latch the selected rs1/rs2\\nvalues into the ID/EX register, and let EX consume stable operands directly.\\nKeep x0 filtering and the existing load-use stall rule intact so freshness\\nstays correct while the hot EX bypass muxes disappear.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread forwarding selects into ID and remove the EX-stage bypass wiring from the top-level path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a forwarding-aware operand latch so ID captures already-selected rs1/rs2 values before the ID/EX flop.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep the compare logic but retarget the bypass outputs to feed ID-side operand selection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the stable operands directly and drop the hot bypass muxes from the EX combinational cone.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread forwarding selects into ID and remove the EX-stage bypass wiring from the top-level path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a forwarding-aware operand latch so ID captures already-selected rs1/rs2 values before the ID/EX flop.\"}, {\"file\": \"rt... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Canonical return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends meaningful time in call/return traffic, and the core\\nalready fast-paths unconditional JAL. A very small return predictor can\\nreuse that existing control-flow machinery to cut recurring JALR flushes\\nwithout dragging a general BTB or a multi-entry stack into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends meaningful time in call/return traffic, and the core\\nalready fast-paths unconditional JAL. A very small return predictor can\\nreuse that existing control-flow machinery to cut recurring JALR flushes\\nwithout dragging a general BTB or a multi-entry stack into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry canonical return predictor that remembers the return\\naddress from the most recent call-like JAL and uses it for fetches of\\nmatching return-like JALR instructions (`rd = x0`, `imm = 0`,\\n`rs1 = x1/x5`). Train the entry from resolved control flow in EX, keep\\nbranches and non-return JALR on the existing EX-resolved path, and only\\nredirect on predictor misses. This should recover the hot library call/\\nreturn pattern with less state and less fetch-side work than the earlier\\nstack and BTB attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the single-entry return predictor lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the return predictor from resolved canonical calls/returns and suppress redundant redirects on correct predictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the single-... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","time":{"start":1778889286158,"end":1778889286298}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09d1070a72400ebc016a07b245d73c819abfe7480432831bbc"}},"id":"prt_e2e106208001lBODEFUsLgd09w","sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a54ffeDPpM1OmIIRz022","messageID":"msg_e2e0fac150010p2nxs0PmNj7tt"}}
