{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778864312665,"sessionID":"ses_1d36e775fffeJ26dHefncIp9Gq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_hoFMSCT2c2EPeSTkIZQCafXn","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\n+title: \"Split divide from ALU\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current ALU keeps the full RV32M divide/remainder logic in the common\n+  combinational datapath. That is expensive logic on a small FPGA, even though\n+  CoreMark is much more branch- and multiply-heavy than divide-heavy. Removing\n+  DIV/REM from the hot ALU cone should raise Fmax without changing the ISA-\n+  visible result stream.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the one-cycle ALU for ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and the multiply ops, but\n+  move DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that only stalls the\n+  pipe when a divide is actually executing. Expose the divide result back\n+  through the existing EX/WB retirement path and preserve the RV32M corner\n+  cases exactly. This is a pure structural split that should shrink the common\n+  critical path while leaving non-divide CoreMark code on the fast path.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n+    description: \"Remove the divider/remainder combinational cone from the common ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/div_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that returns RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider runs.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the divider-busy signal through the top-level stall network so fetch and decode freeze only for active divides.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-002-r4s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\n+title: \"Split divide from ALU\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current ALU keeps the full RV32M divide/remainder logic in the common\n+  combinational datapath. That is expensive logic on a small FPGA, even though\n+  CoreMark is much more branch- and multiply-heavy than divide-heavy. Removing\n+  DIV/REM from the hot ALU cone should raise Fmax without changing the ISA-\n+  visible result stream.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the one-cycle ALU for ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and the multiply ops, but\n+  move DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that only stalls the\n+  pipe when a divide is actually executing. Expose the divide result back\n+  through the existing EX/WB retirement path and preserve the RV32M corner\n+  cases exactly. This is a pure structural split that should shrink the common\n+  critical path while leaving non-divide CoreMark code on the fast path.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n+    description: \"Remove the divider/remainder combinational cone from the common ALU path and keep the fast intege