ral way to\\nshrink the common critical path without changing ISA behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move EX/MEM and MEM/WB bypass selection into ID, latch already-forwarded\\nrs1/rs2 values in the ID/EX register, and let EX consume stable operands\\ndirectly. Keep the existing load-use stall rule and x0 filtering intact so\\ncorrectness stays the same while the EX combinational cone gets shorter.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the bypass-source values into ID and remove the EX-stage forwarding plumbing from the top-level wiring.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve rs1/rs2 forwarding before the ID/EX flop and latch the selected operands into the pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume pre-forwarded operands directly and drop the operand-select muxes from the EX critical path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retime or simplify bypass selection so it serves the ID-side operand latch instead of EX.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the bypass-source values into ID and remove the EX-stage forwarding plumbing from the top-level wiring.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve rs1/rs2 forwardin...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BTB target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark loops revisit the same taken branches many times, but this core\\nstill fetches sequentially until EX resolves control flow. A tiny target\\ncache can turn repeated loop branches into predicted fetches and trim the\\nrecurring wrong-path flush cost without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark loops revisit the same taken branches many times, but this core\\nstill fetches sequentially until EX resolves control flow. A tiny target\\ncache can turn repeated loop branches into predicted fetches and trim the\\nrecurring wrong-path flush cost without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer in IF that stores the\\nPC tag and resolved target for taken B-type branches and JALs. On a BTB\\nhit, fetch the cached target immediately; otherwise fall through as usual.\\nUpdate the entry from the existing EX redirect path so the predictor uses\\ncached targets instead of re-decoding branch immediates in fetch.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add BTB lookup/predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the BTB from resolved taken branches and JAL redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add any small predictor-sideband fields needed to carry BTB state cleanly.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add BTB lookup/predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the BTB from resolved taken branches and JAL redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add any small predictor-sideband fields needed to carry BTB state cleanly.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_p...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Split divide from ALU\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU keeps the full RV32M divide/remainder logic in the common\\ncombinational datapath. That is expensive logic on a small FPGA, even though\\nCoreMark is much more branch- and multiply-heavy than divide-heavy. Removing\\nDIV/REM from the hot ALU cone should raise Fmax without changing the ISA-\\nvisible result stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU keeps the full RV32M divide/remainder logic in the common\\ncombinational datapath. That is expensive logic on a small FPGA, even though\\nCoreMark is much more branch- and multiply-heavy than divide-heavy. Removing\\nDIV/REM from the hot ALU cone should raise Fmax without changing the ISA-\\nvisible result stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the one-cycle ALU for ADD/SUB/logic/shifts and the multiply ops, but\\nmove DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative side unit that only stalls the\\npipe when a divide is actually executing. Expose the divide result back\\nthrough the existing EX/WB retirement path and preserve the RV32M corner\\ncases exactly. This is a pure structural split that should shrink the common\\ncritical path while leaving non-divide CoreMark code on the fast path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the divider/remainder combinational cone from the common ALU path and keep the fast integer/multiply ops single-cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small iterative divider/remainder engine that returns RV32M-correct results and handles divide-by-zero and overflow cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Route DIV/REM instructions into the divider unit and hold the EX/MEM state until the result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an active divide as a pipeline stall source so younger instructions do not advance while the divider r...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Single-entry loop target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark keeps hitting the same inner-loop branch, but this core still\\nwaits for EX to resolve control flow before it can reuse a taken target.\\nA predictor that remembers just the hottest resolved transfer can cut\\nrepeated wrong-path fetches without pulling a general BTB or history RAM\\ninto the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark keeps hitting the same inner-loop branch, but this core still\\nwaits for EX to resolve control flow before it can reuse a taken target.\\nA predictor that remembers just the hottest resolved transfer can cut\\nrepeated wrong-path fetches without pulling a general BTB or history RAM\\ninto the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry hot-branch predictor in IF that stores one recently\\nresolved taken B-type/JAL PC, its target, and a valid bit. On a PC match,\\nfetch the cached target immediately; otherwise fall through and let the\\nexisting EX redirect train the entry from the resolved path. Keep JALR and\\nlegality checks off the predictor fast path so the hardware stays small\\nenough for Tang Nano 20K and the formal state stays bounded.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved PC metadata into IF so the single-entry predictor can train on redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry predictor lookup/update logic and use it to select the next fetch PC.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved PC metadata into IF so the single-entry predictor can train on redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry predictor lookup/update logic and use it to select the next fetch PC.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 335.7, \"delta_pct...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Predecode hot instruction fields\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current decode path fans the full 32-bit instruction into legality,\\nregister address extraction, and hazard checks. On a small FPGA, that\\nrepeated slicing and nested opcode logic is a plausible Fmax limiter once\\nthe branch-target cone has already been trimmed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current decode path fans the full 32-bit instruction into legality,\\nregister address extraction, and hazard checks. On a small FPGA, that\\nrepeated slicing and nested opcode logic is a plausible Fmax limiter once\\nthe branch-target cone has already been trimmed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move instruction-field extraction to the IF/ID boundary: latch opcode,\\nfunct3, funct7, rd, rs1, rs2, and EBREAK qualification in the pipeline\\nbundle, then have `decoder` and `hazard_unit` consume those predecoded\\nfields instead of re-slicing the raw instruction word. This should cut\\ndecode fanout and simplify the common ID critical path without changing\\nISA-visible behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predecoded IF/ID sideband fields for opcode/funct/register-address metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extract and latch the instruction metadata once when fetching, including EBREAK qualification.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrower predecoded fields instead of repeatedly slicing the raw instruction bus.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the latched source register addresses from IF/ID so load-use checks stop fanning out the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the new predecoded bundle through decode and keep the ID/EX register semantics unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg....\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Early branch resolution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage still carries branch compare, redirect selection, and the\\nforwarding muxes on the same path. CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight\\nloops, so taking conditional branches off that cone should help both Fmax\\nand the loop-throughput side of the fitness score.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage still carries branch compare, redirect selection, and the\\nforwarding muxes on the same path. CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight\\nloops, so taking conditional branches off that cone should help both Fmax\\nand the loop-throughput side of the fitness score.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move B-type branch resolution into ID so BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU can\\nredirect one stage earlier, while leaving JALR in EX to preserve the\\nfreshness guarantees on rs1. The common case then stops paying branch\\ncompare cost in EX, and the hot redirect path becomes shorter without\\nchanging architectural results.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can see fresher rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve conditional branch compare and misalignment checks in decode, then emit the early redirect information.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove branch compare work from the hot EX cone and keep JALR plus non-branch ALU work on the live execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire redirect control so the ID-stage branch resolver can drive fetch without waiting for EX on B-type branches.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can see fresher rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"descri...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny return-address stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark and its library calls spend meaningful time in call/return\\ntraffic, and this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch.\\nA bounded return predictor can cut those recurring wrong-path flushes\\nwithout dragging a general BTB or branch-immediate decode into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark and its library calls spend meaningful time in call/return\\ntraffic, and this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch.\\nA bounded return predictor can cut those recurring wrong-path flushes\\nwithout dragging a general BTB or branch-immediate decode into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts JALR returns from\\nthe common link-register pattern and trains only on resolved JAL/JALR\\ncontrol flow. Keep the predictor state bounded and the fetch-side check\\nsimple, so IF uses only opcode/rd/rs1 bits already present in the fetched\\ninstruction while EX remains the source of truth for updates and\\nmispredict recovery.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack and use it to choose the predicted next PC for return-like JALR fetches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate predictor update metadata from resolved JAL/JALR control flow and redirect on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack and use it to choose the predicted next PC for return-like JALR fetches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate predictor update metadata from resolved JAL/JALR control flow and redirect on mispredict...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Dedicated effective-address fast path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Loads, stores, and JALR all currently route base-plus-offset address formation through the same EX datapath that also serves arithmetic and branch work. On a small FPGA, that shared cone is a natural Fmax limiter, especially in a CoreMark mix with frequent memory traffic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Loads, stores, and JALR all currently route base-plus-offset address formation through the same EX datapath that also serves arithmetic and branch work. On a small FPGA, that shared cone is a natural Fmax limiter, especially in a CoreMark mix with frequent memory traffic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated effective-address adder in EX for LOAD, STORE, and JALR, and carry its result as a separate EX/MEM sideband so the main ALU only handles true arithmetic and logic ops. MEM then consumes the sideband directly for address, alignment, and RVFI memory fields. This should shorten the hot execute path without changing architectural retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM sideband field for the dedicated effective-address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute LOAD/STORE/JALR addresses with a separate adder instead of routing them through the main ALU cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the carried effective-address sideband for memory access and RVFI memory reporting.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM sideband field for the dedicated effective-address result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute LOAD/STORE/JALR addresses with a separate adder instead of routing them through the main ALU cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the ...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry hot target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark keeps revisiting the same loop back-edges and a small set of\\ncall/return sites. The prior single-entry hot-target idea shows this class\\nof predictor can pay off, but one slot can thrash when two hot transfers\\nalternate. A second bounded entry could raise hit rate without turning IF\\ninto a general BTB or adding branch-immediate decode to the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark keeps revisiting the same loop back-edges and a small set of\\ncall/return sites. The prior single-entry hot-target idea shows this class\\nof predictor can pay off, but one slot can thrash when two hot transfers\\nalternate. A second bounded entry could raise hit rate without turning IF\\ninto a general BTB or adding branch-immediate decode to the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Expand the hot-transfer predictor into a tiny two-entry cache keyed by the\\nfetch PC, with both entries trained only from resolved taken control-flow\\ntransfers. Keep fetch-side lookup to two PC compares plus a mux, and keep\\ntraining on the existing EX redirect path so no branch-immediate decode or\\nlarge history structure enters IF. The extra slot should preserve the loop\\nback-edge while also remembering a hot call/return site, reducing recurring\\nwrong-path flushes in CoreMark without materially widening the predictor cone.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a two-entry hot-target lookup in fetch and choose the predicted next PC from the matching cached transfer.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train or replace the cache from resolved taken branches and JAL/JALR redirects through the existing redirect path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved transfer metadata between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_i...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark and the runtime library spend real time in call/return traffic,\\nbut this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch. A bounded\\nreturn predictor should cut those recurring wrong-path flushes without\\npulling branch-immediate decode or a general BTB into the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark and the runtime library spend real time in call/return traffic,\\nbut this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch. A bounded\\nreturn predictor should cut those recurring wrong-path flushes without\\npulling branch-immediate decode or a general BTB into the IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry return-address stack that predicts canonical link-\\nregister returns from the fetched opcode/rd/rs1 pattern, and train it only\\nfrom resolved JAL/JALR control flow in EX. Keep B-type branches and non-\\nreturn JALRs on the existing EX-resolved path, so the predictor stays small,\\nformal-friendly, and focused on the call/return behavior CoreMark actually\\nexercises.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 2-entry return-address stack in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate stack push/pop update information from resolved JAL and JALR control flow, and redirect on return mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 2-entry return-address stack in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Gen...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"ID branch resolve\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops, so conditional branch\\nredirects are on a hot path even in a simple in-order core. Moving the\\nB-type compare and redirect decision earlier should shorten the common\\nexecute cone and reduce the cost of taken loop branches.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in tight loops, so conditional branch\\nredirects are on a hot path even in a simple in-order core. Moving the\\nB-type compare and redirect decision earlier should shorten the common\\nexecute cone and reduce the cost of taken loop branches.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Resolve B-type branches in ID using the already-decoded branch target and\\nforwarded source operands, while leaving JALR and all non-branch ALU work\\nin EX. This should remove branch compare and redirect selection from the\\nhot EX path without changing architectural results or the load-use stall\\ndiscipline.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact branch-resolve sideband so the ID-stage decision can flow through the pipeline cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can obtain fresh rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare B-type branches in decode, latch the taken decision and redirect target, and emit the early redirect info.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove conditional branch compare work from EX and keep JALR plus ordinary ALU work on the live execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Merge the ID-stage branch redirect with the existing control-flow path while preserving the RVFI and memory contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark is dominated by tight loops, but this core still fetches pc+4 until\\nEX resolves each branch. A tiny loop-focused predictor can cut repeated\\ntaken-backedge flushes without pulling a general BTB or branch decode into\\nthe IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is dominated by tight loops, but this core still fetches pc+4 until\\nEX resolves each branch. A tiny loop-focused predictor can cut repeated\\ntaken-backedge flushes without pulling a general BTB or branch decode into\\nthe IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a bounded two-entry loop predictor that only learns from resolved taken\\nbackward B-type branches. IF uses a small PC tag compare plus a predicted\\ntarget mux, while EX trains the entry on taken backedges and clears the\\nconfidence on not-taken exits. Limiting the predictor to loop backedges\\nshould capture the hot CoreMark control flow while keeping fetch logic and\\nformal state small.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry loop predictor lookup and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the predictor from resolved branch outcomes and redirect on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the core IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry loop predictor lookup and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the predictor from resolved branch outcomes and redirect on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the core IO contrac...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry backedge cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry exact-match backedge cache in IF and train it only from resolved taken backward B-type branches. Use the cached target on a PC hit, ignore JAL/JALR and forward branches, and keep replacement to a simple bounded policy so the hardware stays small and the fetch cone stays short. This should recover repeated loop backedges with less logic than the earlier general-purpose predictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved ...","metadata":{"matches":17,"truncated":false},"title":"predictor|BTB|RAS|backedge|branch","time":{"start":1778876714311,"end":1778876714318}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0082c6a8ff1ae792016a07812a2df0819880b1d597bc66aa6f"}},"id":"prt_e2d508d45001aUGJ7yqtqs0J05","sessionID":"ses_1d2af83a7ffeN3uktonXlDjd6a","messageID":"msg_e2d507cbe001ECMw2x9HYF6Kg3"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778876714325,"sessionID":"ses_1d2af83a7ffeN3uktonXlDjd6a","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_iqJdTtlVs9pQssI4DJ4QvDJG","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving control-flow target math earlier only works if JALR still sees the freshest rs1 value; precomputing jump targets without airtight forwarding/stalling breaks formal on taken-jump semantics.\n2:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +18.70%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID can materially improve Fmax by cutting the EX-to-IF redirect cone, but the live JALR path still has to stay in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1 data.\n3:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.50%): A tiny static backward-branch predictor can still lose on a loop-heavy benchmark if the branch-immediate decode and legality checks extend the IF critical path more than the saved EX redirect flushes.\n4:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB can make formal blow past wall-clock limits, so fetch-side predictor state needs proof-time budgeting as well as performance tuning.\n5:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, -13.60%): Predecoding opcode/funct/register fields into the IF/ID bundle only helps if decode slicing was the dominant cost; on a small FPGA, widening the fetch-side combinational payload can cost more Fmax than the ID fanout it removes.\n6:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.16%): Carrying predecoded load/store lane metadata (aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign flag) from EX into MEM trims the memory-side combinational cone, but the gain is modest unless that path is actually the timing bottleneck.\n7:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -77.43%): Duplicating effective-address adders only pays off if the shared ALU cone is truly the limiter; on this memory-heavy CoreMark mix, carrying `eff_addr` through EX/MEM added plumbing and fanout without enough critical-path relief.\n8:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +0.00%): A second hot-target slot only helps if it beats the added fetch-side compare/mux cost; CoreMark’s alternating back-edges and call/return sites can still leave a tiny cache net-negative when it does not cut enough wrong-path flushes.\n9:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -22.68%): Reusing the ALU sum for JALR and hoisting `pc + 4` only helps if the execute cone is actually adder-bound; on this core, the saved 32-bit arithmetic was outweighed by the extra mux/fanout on the hot control-flow path.\n10:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (regression, -5.30%): Splitting RVFI fields into a separate retire snapshot only pays off if it actually removes enough width/fanout from the MEM/WB critical cone; if the new snapshot still rides the same retirement boundary, the extra plumbing can erase the timing win.\n11:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.99%): A tiny RAS only pays off if CoreMark’s return traffic is heavy enough to amortize the IF-side opcode/rd/rs1 classification and stack plumbing; otherwise the extra fetch-path work costs more than the JALR flushes it saves.\n12:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -5.21%): Splitting load extraction so `LW` bypasses the shift/extract logic only helps if that variable byte/halfword path is truly on the MEM critical cone; otherwise the added case plumbing and fanout can outweigh the saved shifter on a load-heavy CoreMark mix.\n13:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Moving conditional branch resolution into ID only works if that stage gets the same freshness guarantees as EX, because formal will immediately find stale-operand compares when branch forwarding or load-use stalls are incomplete.\n14:   - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use masks only help when their decode coverage is exact, because skipping a real rs2 dependency in forwarding or load-use logic turns into a register-state formal failure rather than an obvious stall bug.\n\n(End of file - total 14 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving control-flow target math earlier only works if JALR still sees the freshest rs1 value; precomputing jump targets without airtight forwarding/stalling breaks formal on taken-jump semantics.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +18.70%): Precomputing BRANCH/JAL targets in ID can materially improve Fmax by cutting the EX-to-IF redirect cone, but the live JALR path still has to stay in EX because it depends on forwarded rs1 data.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.50%): A tiny static backward-branch predictor can still lose on a loop-heavy benchmark if the branch-immediate decode and legality checks extend the IF critical path more than the saved EX redirect flushes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny direct-mapped BTB can make formal blow past wall-clock limits, so fetch-side predictor state needs proof-time budgeting as well as performance tuning.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, -13.60%): Predecoding opcode/funct/register fields into the IF/ID bundle only helps if decode slicing was the dominant cost; on a small FPGA, widening the fetch-side combinational payload can cost more Fmax than the ID fanout it removes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +1.16%): Carrying predecoded load/store lane metadata (aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign flag) from EX into MEM trims the memory-side combinational cone, but the gain is modest unless that path is actually the timing bottleneck.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -77.43%): Duplicating effective-address adders only pays off if the shared ALU cone is truly the limiter; on this memory-heavy CoreMark mix, carrying `eff_addr` through EX/MEM added plumbing and fanout without enough critical-path relief.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +0.00%): A second hot-target slot only helps if it beats the added fetch-side compare/mux cost; CoreMark’s alternating back-edges and call/return sites can still leave a tiny cache net-negative when it does not cut enough wrong-path flushes.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -22.68%): Reusing the ALU sum for JALR and hoisting `pc + 4` only helps if the execute cone is actually adder-bound; on this core, the saved 32-bit arithmetic was outweighed by the extra mux/fanout on the hot control-flow path.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (regression, -5.30%): Splitting RVFI fields into a separate retire snapshot only pays off if it actually removes enough width/fanout from the MEM/WB critical cone; if the new snapshot still rides the same retirement boundary, the extra plumbing can erase the timing win.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, -9.99%): A tiny RAS only pays off if CoreMark’s return traffic is heavy enough to amortize the IF-side opcode/rd/rs1 classification and stack plumbing; otherwise the extra fetch-path work costs more than the JALR flushes it saves.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -5.21%): Splitting load extraction so `LW` bypasses the shift/extract logic only helps if that variable byte/halfword path is truly on the MEM critical cone; otherwise the added case plumbing and fanout can outweigh the saved shifter on a load-heavy CoreMark mix.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Moving conditional branch resolution into ID only works if that stage gets the same freshness guarantees as EX, because formal will immediately find stale-operand compares when branch forwarding or load-use stalls are incomplete.\n  - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use masks only help when their decode coverage is exact, because skipping a real rs2 dependency in forwarding or load-use logic turns into a register-state formal failure rather than an obvious stall bug.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778876714308,"end":1778876714324}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0082c6a8ff1ae792016a07812a2ddc8198a703aa3658449ad2"}},"id":"prt_e2d508d3b001PdLDHiagEIMr8H","sessionID":"ses_1d2af83a7ffeN3uktonXlDjd6a","messageID":"msg_e2d507cbe001ECMw2x9HYF6Kg3"}}
