{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778879145095,"sessionID":"ses_1d28b9135ffe7A36ywoiVX75Fk","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_N2ubydzeCKo0XFkpztuB8VEU","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r12s0.yaml","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r12s0.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\n2: title: \"Live-source compare gating\"\n3: category: micro_opt\n4: motivation: |\n5:   CoreMark spends much of its time on instructions that do not read both\n6:   source operands, yet the forwarding and load-use logic still evaluates the\n7:   full rs1/rs2 compare trees every cycle. On a small FPGA, that dead equality\n8:   work is a plausible Fmax limiter.\n9: hypothesis: |\n10:   Add compact source-use flags to the decoded control bundle and use them to\n11:   gate only the compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot\n12:   read. Instructions like OP-IMM, loads, JAL, LUI, and AUIPC should skip the\n13:   dead rs2 path entirely while preserving the existing forwarding priority\n14:   and load-use stall rules. This should trim the common ID/EX cone without\n15:   changing architectural behavior.\n16: expected_impact:\n17:   fitness_delta_pct: 4\n18:   confidence: medium\n19: changes:\n20:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n21:     description: \"Add compact source-use flags to the control bundle so later stages can tell whether rs1 and rs2 are live.\"\n22:   - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n23:     description: \"Populate the source-use flags for each opcode class while keeping the existing legality and control decode unchanged.\"\n24:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n25:     description: \"Thread the new source-use flags into the ID/EX register alongside the existing decoded control bundle.\"\n26:   - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n27:     description: \"Gate the rs1/rs2 equality trees with the live-source flags so dead operands do not pay for compare work.\"\n28:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n29:     description: \"Use the same live-source flags to skip unnecessary load-use comparisons for operands the instruction cannot actually read.\"\n\n(End of file - total 29 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\ntitle: \"Live-source compare gating\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark spends much of its time on instructions that do not read both\n  source operands, yet the forwarding and load-use logic still evaluates the\n  full rs1/rs2 compare trees every cycle. On a small FPGA, that dead equality\n  work is a plausible Fmax limiter.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add compact source-use flags to the decoded control bundle and use them to\n  gate only the compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot\n  read. Instructions like OP-IMM, loads, JAL, LUI, and AUIPC should skip the\n  dead rs2 path entirely while preserving the existing forwarding priority\n  and load-use stall rules. This should trim the common ID/EX cone without\n  changing architectural behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r12s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778879145087,"end":1778879145094}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_011fc7fcb6a4bae0016a078aa8fc94819b8c9a5c2fef6a3a19"}},"id":"prt_e2d75a47b001Tb44cTwiwJ43q3","sessionID":"ses_1d28b9135ffe7A36ywoiVX75Fk","messageID":"msg_e2d7596b4001xR4sOnQuCHuJI4"}}
