flow through the pipeline cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget operand selection so ID can obtain fresh rs1/rs2 values for conditional branch compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare B-type branches in decode, latch the taken decision and redirect target, and emit the early redirect info.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove conditional branch compare work from EX and keep JALR plus ordinary ALU work on the live execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Merge the ID-stage branch redirect with the existing control-flow path while preserving the RVFI and memory contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark is dominated by tight loops, but this core still fetches pc+4 until\\nEX resolves each branch. A tiny loop-focused predictor can cut repeated\\ntaken-backedge flushes without pulling a general BTB or branch decode into\\nthe IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is dominated by tight loops, but this core still fetches pc+4 until\\nEX resolves each branch. A tiny loop-focused predictor can cut repeated\\ntaken-backedge flushes without pulling a general BTB or branch decode into\\nthe IF critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a bounded two-entry loop predictor that only learns from resolved taken\\nbackward B-type branches. IF uses a small PC tag compare plus a predicted\\ntarget mux, while EX trains the entry on taken backedges and clears the\\nconfidence on not-taken exits. Limiting the predictor to loop backedges\\nshould capture the hot CoreMark control flow while keeping fetch logic and\\nformal state small.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry loop predictor lookup and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the predictor from resolved branch outcomes and redirect on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the core IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry loop predictor lookup and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the predictor from resolved branch outcomes and redirect on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the core IO contrac...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-entry backedge cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark\\u2019s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny two-entry exact-match backedge cache in IF and train it only from resolved taken backward B-type branches. Use the cached target on a PC hit, ignore JAL/JALR and forward branches, and keep replacement to a simple bounded policy so the hardware stays small and the fetch cone stays short. This should recover repeated loop backedges with less logic than the earlier general-purpose predictor attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Surface the resolved ...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Split MEM read/write cones\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The MEM stage still evaluates load-extraction logic on every memory op, even\\nwhen the instruction is only a store. On a small FPGA, pruning dead work from\\nthe common memory cone is a plausible way to raise Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the MEM combinational work into distinct read and write subpaths so\\nstores never exercise the load shift/extend network and loads never drag the\\nstore-data replication path through the same cone. Keep the architectural\\noutputs identical, but let synthesis see smaller mutually exclusive logic so\\nthe hot memory stage routes and times better.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the load and store combinational branches so each op type only carries the logic it actually needs.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 328.78, \"delta_pct\": -3.19, \"vs_baseline\": 16.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9818, \"ff\": 1960, \"seeds\": [150.99, 147.67, 133.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-15T20:33:43.049217Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"scribe_skipped\": \"PermissionError: scribe touched off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cor...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed fetch boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot path is likely the combinational fetch-to-decode loop,\\nwhere `imem_data` still fans into decode, hazard checks, and PC control in\\nthe same cycle. Breaking that boundary should give synthesis a cleaner\\nstage cut than another fetch-side predictor, especially after the branch\\ntarget work already moved earlier.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a real IF/ID register so fetched instructions are latched once\\nbefore decode and hazard logic see them. That should shorten the\\nimem-to-decode-to-flush feedback cone and reduce the amount of logic that\\nsits on the fetch critical path, while preserving the architectural\\nretirement stream and existing stall/redirect rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retimes fetch through a registered IF/ID boundary so the PC update path no longer feeds decode combinationally in the same cycle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched fetch bundle as the decode input and keep the existing decode, operand read, and ID/EX semantics aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered IF/ID view for load-use checks so stall generation no longer sits on the same combinational fetch path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level stage boundary for the new fetch register while preserving the RVFI and memory I/O contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Short backedge delta predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in short inner loops, but the core\\nfetches sequentially until EX resolves each taken backedge. Prior predictor\\nattempts were too general and widened the fetch cone; a smaller, loop-only\\npredictor with a compact state footprint could recover the recurring flush\\ncost without dragging a full BTB or return stack into IF.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny exact-match predictor for short taken backward B-type branches\\nonly. Train it from resolved EX redirects, but store a compact signed delta\\nrather than a full target so the fetch-side datapath stays narrow; if the\\nbranch offset does not fit the compressed form, fall back to the existing\\nEX-resolved path. Keep JAL/JALR and forward branches off the predictor fast\\npath so the hardware remains bounded and formal stays tractable.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the short-backedge lookup, compressed delta storage, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the resolved branch metadata needed to train or invalidate the predictor on taken backedges and mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update path between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the...\n  Line 34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"JAL fast-path predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts added too much fetch-side state or compare logic and\\nlost more Fmax than they saved in flushes. CoreMark still pays a full EX-\\nresolved redirect on every JAL call, so a narrower unconditional predictor\\nmay recover some of that control-flow cost without dragging in BTB or RAS\\nmachinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict unconditional JALs in IF by decoding only the JAL opcode and\\ncomputing the J-type target on the fetch side. Carry the predicted next PC\\nalongside the instruction so EX can suppress the redundant redirect when the\\ntarget matches, leaving JALR and conditional branches on the existing path.\\nThis targets call traffic with far less state than the failed BTB/RAS style\\npredictors.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact prediction sideband fields to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so fetch can carry a predicted next PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize JAL in fetch, compute its target immediately, and mark the prediction as valid for the downstream stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the prediction sideband with the decoded instruction so EX can compare the predicted and resolved next PCs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the redundant redirect on correctly predicted JALs while keeping JALR and branch resolution unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Live-source compare gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends much of its time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource operands, yet the forwarding and load-use logic still evaluates the\\nfull rs1/rs2 compare trees every cycle. On a small FPGA, that dead equality\\nwork is a plausible Fmax limiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends much of its time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource operands, yet the forwarding and load-use logic still evaluates the\\nfull rs1/rs2 compare trees every cycle. On a small FPGA, that dead equality\\nwork is a plausible Fmax limiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add compact source-use flags to the decoded control bundle and use them to\\ngate only the compare trees for operands the current instruction cannot\\nread. Instructions like OP-IMM, loads, JAL, LUI, and AUIPC should skip the\\ndead rs2 path entirely while preserving the existing forwarding priority\\nand load-use stall rules. This should trim the common ID/EX cone without\\nchanging architectural behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact source-use flags to the control bundle so later stages can tell whether rs1 and rs2 are live.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the source-use flags for each opcode class while keeping the existing legality and control decode unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the new source-use flags into the ID/EX register alongside the existing decoded control bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate the rs1/rs2 equality trees with the live-source flags so dead operands do not pay for compare work.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the same live-source flags to skip unnecessary load-use comparisons for operands the instruction cannot actually read.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Add a fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\\nhazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\\nsmall FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\\nA tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\\narchitectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The front end still lets `imem_data` fan into PC selection, decode, and\\nhazard checks in one cycle. That creates a wide fetch-to-decode cone on a\\nsmall FPGA, even after the earlier branch-target and predictor experiments.\\nA tiny registered fetch buffer should cut that cone without changing the\\narchitectural retirement stream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry fetch buffer that latches the fetched `pc`, `instr`, and\\nvalid bit before decode consumes them, while keeping the PC engine and\\nredirect logic on a narrower path. This should decouple the fetch timing\\nfrom the decode/hazard fanout, reduce the hot combinational span through the\\nfront end, and preserve the existing stall, flush, and RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the buffered fetch bundle unchanged so decode and immediate generation stay aligned to the new boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewire the top-level front end around the buffered fetch stage without changing the external I/O or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the one-entry fetch buffer and separate the PC update path from the decode-facing output bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", ...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Canonical return stack\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic, but the core only\\nfast-paths unconditional JAL today. A tiny return predictor can remove the\\nrecurring JALR redirections from library calls without pulling a general BTB\\nor wider branch history into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic, but the core only\\nfast-paths unconditional JAL today. A tiny return predictor can remove the\\nrecurring JALR redirections from library calls without pulling a general BTB\\nor wider branch history into the fetch cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry return-address stack that trains only on canonical calls\\nusing x1/x5 link registers and predicts only canonical return JALR patterns\\n(rs1 = x1/x5, imm = 0, rd = x0). Keep conditional branches and non-return\\nJALR on the existing EX-resolved path, and reuse the existing predicted-next-\\nPC sideband so IF can select the return target without adding a general target\\ncache. This should trim CoreMark's call/return flushes while keeping the\\npredictor state tiny and bounded.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small return-stack lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate return-stack push/pop update signals from resolved JAL and JALR control flow, and redirect on return mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread the predictor update metadata between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small return-stack lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like...","metadata":{"matches":26,"truncated":false},"title":"predictor|BTB|RAS|JAL|branch","time":{"start":1778884117224,"end":17788841172