{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778884190635,"sessionID":"ses_1d23e8f85ffedgjumT7d2WjFCh","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_LRjzOfjiMcqedxsu0PuVvfQK","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r13s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\n+title: \"Canonical call/return fast-path\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  The JAL fast-path already showed that cheap control-flow speculation can\n+  improve this core. CoreMark still spends real time in call/return traffic,\n+  so a bounded return predictor is the next reusable control-flow pattern to\n+  attack without introducing a general BTB.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Keep the current JAL fetch-side prediction, and add a tiny canonical\n+  return predictor that only tracks x1/x5 link-register returns. IF should\n+  use the remembered return target immediately for matching JALR fetches,\n+  while EX remains the source of truth for training and mispredict recovery.\n+  This should recover recurring call/return flushes more cheaply than a\n+  general target cache and stay small enough for Tang Nano 20K.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the canonical return lookup in fetch and feed its predicted next PC into the existing control-flow path.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the return predictor from resolved JAL/JALR control flow and redirect on mispredicts only.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r13s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r