{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778886275770,"sessionID":"ses_1d21e7b59ffeCWf9l077nlP3Hu","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_lqzFKrlnMdaSP5ELVHzCwIkx","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\n+title: \"Canonical return predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends meaningful time in call/return traffic, and the core\n+  already fast-paths unconditional JAL. A very small return predictor can\n+  reuse that existing control-flow machinery to cut recurring JALR flushes\n+  without dragging a general BTB or a multi-entry stack into the fetch cone.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a single-entry canonical return predictor that remembers the return\n+  address from the most recent call-like JAL and uses it for fetches of\n+  matching return-like JALR instructions (`rd = x0`, `imm = 0`,\n+  `rs1 = x1/x5`). Train the entry from resolved control flow in EX, keep\n+  branches and non-return JALR on the existing EX-resolved path, and only\n+  redirect on predictor misses. This should recover the hot library call/\n+  return pattern with less state and less fetch-side work than the earlier\n+  stack and BTB attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the single-entry return predictor lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return JALR instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the return predictor from resolved canonical calls/returns and suppress redundant redirects on correct predictions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r14s2.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\n+title: \"Canonical return predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends meaningful time in call/return traffic, and the core\n+  already fast-paths unconditional JAL. A very small return predictor can\n+  reuse that existing control-flow machinery to cut recurring JALR flushes\n+  without dragging a general BTB or a multi-entry stack into the fetch cone.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a single-entry canonical return predictor that remembers the return\n+  address from the most recent call-like JAL and uses it for fetches of\n+  matching return-like JALR instructions (`rd = x0`, `imm = 0`,\n+  `rs1 = x1/x5`). Train the entry from resolved control flow in EX, keep\n+  branches and non-return JALR on the existing EX-resolved path, and only\n+  redirect on predictor misses. This should recover the hot library call/\n+  return pattern with less state and less fetch-side work than the earlier\n+  stack and BTB attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the single-entry return predictor lookup in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return JALR instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Train the return predictor from resolved canonical calls/returns and suppress redundant redirects on correct predictions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core