{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778889339696,"sessionID":"ses_1d1f07a00ffeT1osGLt5io7pEn","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_uwfIi9cXROTgAo14ghGd9QyM","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r15s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r15s2\n+title: \"Static backedge predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loop backedges, but the current\n+  front end only fast-paths JAL. A predictor that stays limited to backward\n+  B-type branches can recover the recurring taken-loop penalty without dragging\n+  a BTB, RAS, or per-PC history table into the fetch cone.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny static backedge predictor in IF that treats B-type branches with\n+  negative displacement as taken and otherwise falls through. Reuse the existing\n+  predicted-next-PC sideband so EX can suppress only correct redirects and still\n+  train the architectural path on mispredicts. This targets the hot CoreMark\n+  loop branches with much less state than the failed cache and stack predictors.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: low\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the opcode-gated static backedge prediction in fetch and drive the predicted next PC through the existing sideband.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Compare resolved branch targets against the predicted next PC and suppress redirects on correct backedge predictions.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r15s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r15s2.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/benc