{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778838352904,"sessionID":"ses_1d4f93f9bffe3Fp2EelXHrbx2U","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_G5vhilL9AcnEMwQFDxDQ5VyR","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl","offset":1,"limit":80},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest (no implementation change)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Used by the orchestrator integration test (--from-hypothesis path).\\nMeasures the unchanged baseline through the full eval pipeline so the\\nresulting log entry locks in the first fitness number for phase 8.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"No RTL changes \\u2014 exercises the worktree -> emit_verilog -> formal ->\\ncosim -> fpga path on the existing rtl/*.sv to confirm the orchestrator\\nproduces a structured log entry.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 0, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Sentinel \\u2014 no real change. skip_implementation:true below bypasses\\nthe implementation agent, so the file path here is metadata only;\\nit just has to satisfy the schema's rtl/<file> pattern. core.sv is\\nalways present and untouched.\\n\"}], \"skip_implementation\": true, \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 301.04, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"lut4\": 9880, \"ff\": 1858, \"seeds\": [121.29, 135.21, 136.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T02:16:49.012016+00:00\"}\n2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsmall iterative RV32M divide/remainder unit and add an EX-stage interlock\\nonly when one of those opcodes is in flight. Leave ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\\nbranches, loads/stores, jumps, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the current\\nsingle-cycle path. The timed CoreMark loop should pay no meaningful IPC\\npenalty because it does not execute DIV/REM, while the FPGA critical path\\nshould shrink from divider-through-ALU-mux to the existing add/compare/mul\\npaths. Preserve exact RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics and\\nkeep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/REM hardware from the normal ALU result\\nmux, retaining single-cycle non-divide ALU and MUL behavior plus the\\nexisting A... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-Branch Taken Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-light static branch predictor in IF that recognizes B-type instructions and predicts backward branches taken when the sign bit of the branch immediate is set. Compute the B immediate in IF, redirect the next PC to pc+imm for predicted-taken backward branches, and carry predicted_taken plus predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compare the actual branch result and target against the prediction, redirect only on mispredict, and set rvfi pc_wdata to the actual next PC as today. JAL/JALR remain resolved in EX to keep the predictor slot focused on conditional branches and avoid adding a return-address stack or BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8.0, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target fields so EX can distinguish correct predictions f... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-004-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Single Shared RV32M Multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three unconditional multiplier expressions in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsingle shared 33-bit-by-33-bit product. For MUL, zero-extend both operands and\\nuse the low 32 bits; for MULH, sign-extend both operands; for MULHSU,\\nsign-extend rs1 and zero-extend rs2; for MULHU, zero-extend both operands.\\nSelect product[63:32] for the high-half operations and product[31:0] for MUL.\\nKeep the RISC... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-005-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a direct load-result forwarding path from the MEM stage's existing sign/zero-extended load_data to the EX operand muxes. When an instruction in EX consumes the rd of a load currently in MEM, select this MEM load result for rs1 and/or rs2 instead of stalling for the value to appear in MEM/WB. If io_dmemReady is low for that load, keep the current dmem hold behavior and suppress any younger EX redirect until the load data is valid; once ready, the dependent instruction executes in the same cycle the load ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-006-r2s2\", \"title\": \"IF Direct-Jump Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing IF-stage predictor from backward conditional branches to\\ndirect JAL targets. Recognize opcode 1101111 in IF, form the J immediate,\\npredict pc+imm as the next PC when the target is word-aligned, and carry the\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata with the instruction. In\\nEX, make JAL redirect mispredict-only by comparing the resolved jump target\\nagainst the carried prediction; JALR remains unpredicted and still redirects\\nfrom EX. This should remove the two-cycle recovery penalty from correctly\\npredicted direct jumps and calls while preserving RVFI pc_wdata, misaligned\\njump traps, and the single-retire c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n8: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-Aware Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the hazard_unit load-use predicate so it only compares the load rd\\nagainst source fields that the IF/ID instruction actually reads. Compute\\nsimple uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits from the IF/ID opcode: rs1 is used by\\nOP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 is used only by... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Static-Biased Branch Agree Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry, PC-indexed, two-bit \\\"agree\\\" predictor for conditional branch\\ndirection. IF still computes the existing static BTFNT direction and branch\\ntarget from the fetched instruction; the table counter predicts whether the\\ndynamic outcome will agree with that static direction. Initialize counters to\\nstrongly agree so cold branches behave exactly like the current predictor.\\nFor legal aligned B-type branches, predict taken when the table prediction\\ncombined with the static direction says taken, and carry the existing\\npredicted_taken/predicted_target metadata into EX. Direct JAL prediction\\nremains unchanged, and JALR remains ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-004-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Hot-Bit ALU Result Mux\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is now Fmax-sensitive in the EX-stage ALU path. The\\nALU operation encoding is dense across both common RV32I operations and the\\nRV32M operations, and rtl/alu.sv selects every result through one broad case\\nstatement. Recent experiments showed that touching the multiplier primitive\\nitself is risky: the 33x33 shared-multiplier rewrite preserved IPC but badly\\nhurt Fmax. A safer micro-optimization is to leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand pipeline handshake unchanged, but make the ALU control encoding expose a\\nhot/cold split so synthesis can build a shallower result mux for the common\\ninteger/address/branch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is now Fmax-sensitive in the EX-stage ALU path. The\\nALU operation encoding is dense across both common RV32I operations and the\\nRV32M operations, and rtl/alu.sv selects every result through one broad case\\nstatement. Recent experiments showed that touching the multiplier primitive\\nitself is risky: the 33x33 shared-multiplier rewrite preserved IPC but badly\\nhurt Fmax. A safer micro-optimization is to leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand pipeline handshake unchanged, but make the ALU control encoding expose a\\nhot/cold split so synthesis can build a shallower result mux for the common\\ninteger/address/branch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode ALU op constants so bit 4 is a registered \\\"M-extension operation\\\"\\ndiscriminator: all RV32I/simple operations keep op[4]=0, while MUL/MULH/\\nMULHSU/MULHU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU move to op[4]=1 with op[2:0] matching the\\nRV32M funct3 order. Refactor rtl/alu.sv into a hot simple-result mux for\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI and a separate M-result mux for the existing\\nmultiply/divide/remainder expressions, then select between them with op[4].\\nThis should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common EX/MEM alu_result\\npath without cha... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-005-r4s1\", \"title\": \"ID-Stage JALR Redirect\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of