ominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-018-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Biased Static Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor angle is therefore a zero-state refinement\\nof the static conditional-branch rule. A quick dynamic profile of the timed\\nCoreMark region shows that the baseline BTFNT rule is especially wrong for\\ntwo simple branch classes: backward BEQ branches, which are mostly not taken\\nin this binary, and forward BNE branches, which are usually taken. Those two\\nclasses account for a large fraction of residual static predictor mistakes,\\nand they can be recognized from the funct3 bits already examined in IF for\\nbranch legality. This targets iterations/cycle without adding a predictor\\nRAM, update feedback path, BTB, RAS, or extra target adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining p... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-25. Use offset=26 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest (no implementation change)\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Used by the orchestrator integration test (--from-hypothesis path).\\nMeasures the unchanged baseline through the full eval pipeline so the\\nresulting log entry locks in the first fitness number for phase 8.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"No RTL changes \\u2014 exercises the worktree -> emit_verilog -> formal ->\\ncosim -> fpga path on the existing rtl/*.sv to confirm the orchestrator\\nproduces a structured log entry.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 0, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Sentinel \\u2014 no real change. skip_implementation:true below bypasses\\nthe implementation agent, so the file path here is metadata only;\\nit just has to satisfy the schema's rtl/<file> pattern. core.sv is\\nalways present and untouched.\\n\"}], \"skip_implementation\": true, \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 301.04, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"lut4\": 9880, \"ff\": 1858, \"seeds\": [121.29, 135.21, 136.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T02:16:49.012016+00:00\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline ALU places RV32M multiplication and division in the same\\ncombinational EX-stage result mux as common integer operations. A 32-bit\\ncombinational divider is very likely to dominate LUT depth and area on\\nthe Tang Nano 20K, yet CoreMark's timed region is matrix/list/state code\\nthat is multiply-heavy and effectively division-free. Keeping DIV/REM\\nsingle-cycle optimizes an uncommon path while lowering Fmax for every\\ninstruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsmall iterative RV32M divide/remainder unit and add an EX-stage interlock\\nonly when one of those opcodes is in flight. Leave ADD/SUB/logic/shift,\\nbranches, loads/stores, jumps, and MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the current\\nsingle-cycle path. The timed CoreMark loop should pay no meaningful IPC\\npenalty because it does not execute DIV/REM, while the FPGA critical path\\nshould shrink from divider-through-ALU-mux to the existing add/compare/mul\\npaths. Preserve exact RV32M divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 semantics and\\nkeep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions for formal builds.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational DIV/REM hardware from the normal ALU result\\nmux, retaining single-cycle non-divide ALU and MUL behavior plus the\\nexisting A... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\nno-stall path and branch target... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Backward-Branch Taken Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-light static branch predictor in IF that recognizes B-type instructions and predicts backward branches taken when the sign bit of the branch immediate is set. Compute the B immediate in IF, redirect the next PC to pc+imm for predicted-taken backward branches, and carry predicted_taken plus predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compare the actual branch result and target against the prediction, redirect only on mispredict, and set rvfi pc_wdata to the actual next PC as today. JAL/JALR remain resolved in EX to keep the predictor slot focused on conditional branches and avoid adding a return-address stack or BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8.0, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target fields so EX can distinguish correct predictions f... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-004-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Single Shared RV32M Multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same