e gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a direct load-result forwarding path from the MEM stage's existing sign/zero-extended load_data to the EX operand muxes. When an instruction in EX consumes the rd of a load currently in MEM, select this MEM load result for rs1 and/or rs2 instead of stalling for the value to appear in MEM/WB. If io_dmemReady is low for that load, keep the current dmem hold behavior and suppress any younger EX redirect until the load data is valid; once ready, the dependent instruction executes in the same cycle the load ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-006-r2s2\", \"title\": \"IF Direct-Jump Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing IF-stage predictor from backward conditional branches to\\ndirect JAL targets. Recognize opcode 1101111 in IF, form the J immediate,\\npredict pc+imm as the next PC when the target is word-aligned, and carry the\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata with the instruction. In\\nEX, make JAL redirect mispredict-only by comparing the resolved jump target\\nagainst the carried prediction; JALR remains unpredicted and still redirects\\nfrom EX. This should remove the two-cycle recovery penalty from correctly\\npredicted direct jumps and calls while preserving RVFI pc_wdata, misaligned\\njump traps, and the single-retire c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-Aware Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the hazard_unit load-use predicate so it only compares the load rd\\nagainst source fields that the IF/ID instruction actually reads. Compute\\nsimple uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits from the IF/ID opcode: rs1 is used by\\nOP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 is used only by... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Static-Biased Branch Agree Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 64-entry, PC-indexed, two-bit \\\"agree\\\" predictor for conditional branch\\ndirection. IF still computes the existing static BTFNT direction and branch\\ntarget from the fetched instruction; the table counter predicts whether the\\ndynamic outcome will agree with that static direction. Initialize counters to\\nstrongly agree so cold branches behave exactly like the current predictor.\\nFor legal aligned B-type branches, predict taken when the table prediction\\ncombined with the static direction says taken, and carry the existing\\npredicted_taken/predicted_target metadata into EX. Direct JAL prediction\\nremains unchanged, and JALR remains ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-004-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Hot-Bit ALU Result Mux\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is now Fmax-sensitive in the EX-stage ALU path. The\\nALU operation encoding is dense across both common RV32I operations and the\\nRV32M operations, and rtl/alu.sv selects every result through one broad case\\nstatement. Recent experiments showed that touching the multiplier primitive\\nitself is risky: the 33x33 shared-multiplier rewrite preserved IPC but badly\\nhurt Fmax. A safer micro-optimization is to leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand pipeline handshake unchanged, but make the ALU control encoding expose a\\nhot/cold split so synthesis can build a shallower result mux for the common\\ninteger/address/branch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is now Fmax-sensitive in the EX-stage ALU path. The\\nALU operation encoding is dense across both common RV32I operations and the\\nRV32M operations, and rtl/alu.sv selects every result through one broad case\\nstatement. Recent experiments showed that touching the multiplier primitive\\nitself is risky: the 33x33 shared-multiplier rewrite preserved IPC but badly\\nhurt Fmax. A safer micro-optimization is to leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand pipeline handshake unchanged, but make the ALU control encoding expose a\\nhot/cold split so synthesis can build a shallower result mux for the common\\ninteger/address/branch work.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode ALU op constants so bit 4 is a registered \\\"M-extension operation\\\"\\ndiscriminator: all RV32I/simple operations keep op[4]=0, while MUL/MULH/\\nMULHSU/MULHU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU move to op[4]=1 with op[2:0] matching the\\nRV32M funct3 order. Refactor rtl/alu.sv into a hot simple-result mux for\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI and a separate M-result mux for the existing\\nmultiply/divide/remainder expressions, then select between them with op[4].\\nThis should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common EX/MEM alu_result\\npath without cha... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-005-r4s1\", \"title\": \"ID-Stage JALR Redirect\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an early JALR redirect path in ID for cases where the rs1 base is already\\navailable from the regfile or same-cycle WB write-first bypass. ID computes\\nthe JALR target from rs1 plus the I-immediate, verifies the aligned target,\\nand sends a PC redirect to IF in the same cycle the JALR enters ID/EX. The\\nID/EX prediction metadata is overwritten with that resolved target so the\\nlater EX-stage instance of the same JALR does not redirect a second time.\\nWhen rs1 is waiting on an older instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM, or when the\\nbackend is ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-006-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Return-Address Stack JALR Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, which removes most loop-back and direct-call\\ncontrol bubbles. The remaining obvious predictor hole is JALR: every return\\nstill resolves in EX and forces the same wrong-path flush even though CoreMark\\nuses direct calls paired with register-indirect returns. A tiny return-address\\nstack targets that specific loss with lower timing risk than another indexed\\nbranch table, because IF only needs to recognize the canonical return\\nencoding and mux in a registered stack top.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, which removes most loop-back and direct-call\\ncontrol bubbles. The remaining obvious predictor hole is JALR: every return\\nstill resolves in EX and forces the same wrong-path flush even though CoreMark\\nuses direct calls paired with register-indirect returns. A tiny return-address\\nstack targets that specific loss with lower timing risk than another indexed\\nbranch table, because IF only needs to recognize the canonical return\\nencoding and mux in a registered stack top.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small non-speculative return-address stack for link-register control\\nflow. When a valid non-trapping JAL or JALR call with rd=x1 or rd=x5 advances\\nthrough EX, push pc+4 into a 4-entry stack. When a valid canonical return\\nJALR with