ior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite `rtl/mem_stage.sv` to decode `in.alu_result[1:0]` explicitly for\\nbyte/halfword steering instead of using variable shifts. Build `byte_val`,\\n`hword_val`, `byte_mask`, and replicated store data from small `case` tables\\nover the low address bits and access width. Preserve the existing misalignment\\ntrap rules, dmem gating on traps, RVFI aligned `mem_addr`, raw `mem_rdata`,\\nreplicated `mem_wdata`, and exact load sign/zero extension semantics. The\\nexpected gain is a mode... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-008-r5s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by