 immediate in IF, redirect the next PC to pc+imm for predicted-taken backward branches, and carry predicted_taken plus predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compare the actual branch result and target against the prediction, redirect only on mispredict, and set rvfi pc_wdata to the actual next PC as today. JAL/JALR remain resolved in EX to keep the predictor slot focused on conditional branches and avoid adding a return-address stack or BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8.0, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target fields so EX can distinguish correct predictions f...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-004-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Single Shared RV32M Multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three unconditional multiplier expressions in rtl/alu.sv with a\\nsingle shared 33-bit-by-33-bit product. For MUL, zero-extend both operands and\\nuse the low 32 bits; for MULH, sign-extend both operands; for MULHSU,\\nsign-extend rs1 and zero-extend rs2; for MULHU, zero-extend both operands.\\nSelect product[63:32] for the high-half operations and product[31:0] for MUL.\\nKeep the RISC...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260427-005-r2s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed