 predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, and the latest winning change moved cold DIV/REM\\noff the critical ALU path. The predictor slot should therefore avoid broad\\nlearned branch tables that have already shown a bad cycle-count versus Fmax\\ntradeoff: the 64-entry agree table and 16-entry forward override both reduced\\nCoreMark cycles but lost more clock than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining high-value predictor miss is JALR return control flow. The\\n4-entry RAS experiment showed returns exist in the timed path, but its stack\\nbookkeeping and IF routing were still too expensive. A single tagged return\\ntarget entry is a narrower bet: it targets recurring canonical return PCs\\nwith one PC tag, one target register, and one IF compare, avoiding a BTB,\\nmulti-entry stack, or conditional-branch table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches and\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, and the latest winning change moved cold DIV/REM\\noff the critical ALU path. The predictor slot should therefore avoid broad\\nlearned branch tables that have already shown a bad cycle-count versus Fmax\\ntradeoff: the 64-entry agree table and 16-entry forward override both reduced\\nCoreMark cycles but lost more clock than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining high-value predictor miss is JALR return control flow. The\\n4-entry RAS experiment showed returns exist in the timed path, but its stack\\nbookkeeping and IF routing were still too expensive. A single tagged return\\ntarget entry is a narrower bet: it targets recurring canonical return PCs\\nwith one PC tag, one target register, and one IF compare, avoiding a BTB,\\nmulti-entry stack, or conditional-branch table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry return target predictor for canonical return JALRs. When EX\\nresolves a valid...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-018-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Biased Static Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor angle is therefore a zero-state refinement\\nof the static conditional-branch rule. A quick dynamic profile of the timed\\nCoreMark region shows that the baseline BTFNT rule is especially wrong for\\ntwo simple branch classes: backward BEQ branches, which are mostly not taken\\nin this binary, and forward BNE branches, which are usually taken. Those two\\nclasses account for a large fraction of residual static predictor mistakes,\\nand they can be recognized from the funct3 bits already examined in IF for\\nbranch legality. This targets iterations/cycle without adding a predictor\\nRAM, update feedback path, BTB, RAS, or extra target adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best frontend already has the two predictor features that have\\nactually paid off: backward conditional branches predict taken, and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned predictor attempts point in the\\nother direction: both the 64-entry agree table and the 16-entry forward-only\\noverride table reduced CoreMark cycles, but their table read/update routing\\ncost more Fmax than the saved mispredicts recovered. Return prediction was\\neven less attractive after the post-DIV timing win, with only small cycle\\nsavings and a large clock regression.\\n\\nThe remaining p...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-019-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Hot-Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline control unchanged, but make the opcode encoding expose a\\nsimple hot/cold split that synthesis can map into a shallower mux for the\\ncommon RV32I path. This targets median Fmax with no CoreMark cycle-count\\nrisk and avoids the multiplier-primitive rewrites that previously regressed\\nbadly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already moved DIV/REM out of the universal EX\\ndatapath and added the one-deep store slot, so the remaining micro-optimum\\nis likely in the hot single-cycle EX logic rather than in another stall\\nhiding structure. `rtl/alu.sv` still selects every RV32I operation and all\\nmultiply-class results through one dense ALU opcode case. Even though\\nDIV/REM now return dummy zero in the ALU, the common ADD/address/compare/\\nshift path still shares decode and mux structure with the cold M-extension\\nresult cases.\\n\\nA previous \\\"hot-bit ALU\\\" attempt was never architecturally evaluated because\\nthe implementation touched an off-limits non-RTL helper file. The idea\\nremains attractive after the divider win: leave every arithmetic primitive\\nand all pipeline contr...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-020-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Store-Slot-Aware Fetch Skid Queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. The better\\nstructural angle now is narrower: use a tiny fetch skid queue only to absorb\\ninstruction-memory backpressure and to keep fetch productive while the new\\nstore slot is draining for older stores. This targets the measured\\nstore-slot cycle win's remaining companion bottleneck without touching the\\nhot EX operand path, load data forwarding, branch predictor state, or RVFI\\nretirement width.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design combines the two changes that have paid off most:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and a one-deep pending-store slot\\nlets a stalled normal-RAM store retire while the physical dmem write drains\\nlater. That store slot reduced CoreMark cycles, but the frontend is still\\nstructurally brittle under the tournament's random imem stalls: `if_stage`\\npresents live imem data directly to decode, and `hazard_unit` still turns\\nraw `io_imemReady=0` into a global pipeline stall.\\n\\nA previous generic elastic fetch buffer regressed because it added permanent\\nfrontend queue/arbitration cost before the divider and store-slot timing\\nwins, and it tried to prefetch through all backend stalls. T...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-021-r9s2\", \"title\": \"X1 Last-Call Return Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already has the predictor features that have proven\\nuseful without excessive timing cost: backward conditional branches are\\npredicted taken and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF. Broader learned\\ndirection tables did reduce CoreMark cycles, but the IF table read and EX\\nupdate routing cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. The return\\npredictor attempts also showed that return bubbles exist, but the 4-entry\\nstack and the PC-tagged one-entry cache added too much frontend comparison,\\nbookkeeping, and routing.\\n\\nThe narrow remaining predictor opportunity is to keep only the useful part\\nof a return-address stack: the standard ABI link register x1. CoreMark is\\ncompiled as ordinary RV32IM C code, so direct calls write x1 and returns are\\ncanonical `jalr x0, 0(x1)`. A single last-call target register can predict\\nthe common innermost return without a BTB, tag compare, multi-entry stack, or\\nconditional-branch table. It intentionally gives up nested-return coverage in\\nexchange for a much smaller IF critical-path footprint.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already has the predictor features that have proven\\nuseful without excessive timing cost: backward conditional branches are\\npredicted taken and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF. Broader learned\\ndirection tables did reduce CoreMark cycles, but the IF table read and EX\\nupdate routing cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. The return\\npredictor attempts also showed that return bubbles exist, but the 4-entry\\nstack and the PC-tagged one-entry cache added too much frontend comparison,\\nbookkeeping, and routing.\\n\\nThe narrow remaining predictor opportunity is to keep only the useful part\\nof a return-address stack: the standard ABI link register x1. CoreMark is\\ncompiled as ordinary RV32IM C code, so direct calls write x1 and r...\n  Line 29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-022-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Collapse Redundant EX/MEM Metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycles, bus behavior,\\nbranch prediction, RVFI ordering, or any ISA-visible result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL already captured the large available wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is out of the one-cycle ALU path, and the pending store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. The remaining micro-opt slot should therefore\\navoid new predictors, queues, or arithmetic units and instead trim logic that\\nis already proven unnecessary on the hot pipeline boundary.\\n\\nThe EX/MEM bundle still carries several fields that do not add architectural\\ninformation. `write_data` is always assigned the same post-forwarded rs2\\nvalue as `rs2_val`, while `branch_taken` and `branch_target` are registered\\nbut never consumed by MEM, WB, RVFI, forwarding, or hazard logic. Those bits\\nadd about 65 EX/MEM flops plus mux/load on the EX branch-target and rs2 data\\npaths, exactly where the post-DIV design is trying to preserve Fmax. Pruning\\nthis redundant metadata should not change CoreMark cycl...\n  Line 30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-023-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-imem path whenever the external\\nbus is ready, and consult a tiny cached copy only on cycles where imem would\\notherwise stall. CoreMark's timed code repeatedly executes the same matrix,\\nlist, state, CRC, and loop-control instructions over a 10-iteration run, so\\neven a small direct-mapped instruction replay table should turn many random\\niStall bubbles into normal fetch cycles after warmup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM datapath from\\nEX and added a one-deep pending-store slot, so the remaining structural\\nloss is less about store backpressure and more about the instruction side\\nof the tournament's random bus model. Today `io_imemReady=0` still becomes\\na global frontend stall: the PC holds, IF emits a bubble, and decode cannot\\nuse an instruction even if the same hot-loop PC was fetched many times\\nearlier.\\n\\nThe previous fetch skid queues tried to decouple live imem traffic from\\ndecode, but they added permanent queue and PC-control logic on the zero-wait\\npath and lost too much Fmax. A better structural angle is a stall-only\\nreplay cache: keep the current direct live-im...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-024-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Single-Adder Static Control Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms branch and JAL targets with two separate `pc + imm` adders\\nand uses the full adder results for target-alignment gating before choosing\\nthe next PC. Since B- and J-type immediates always have bit 0 clear, the\\ntarget alignment test can be derived from the encoded imm[1] bit and the PC\\nlow bits without waiting for a 32-bit target adder. Branch and JAL are also\\nmutually exclusive opcodes, so one selected-immediate target adder is enough.\\n\\nThis attacks predictor-path Fmax with no intended CoreMark cycle-count risk:\\nthe set of predicted conditional branches and direct JALs remains the same\\nas the current best design for all aligned PCs, and EX still repairs any\\nwrong prediction through the existing resolved-next-PC mismatch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor features are the simple ones that already paid\\nfor themselves: backward conditional branches are predicted taken and direct\\nJAL targets are predicted in IF. The learned direction tables and return\\npredictors reduced CoreMark cycles slightly, but each added enough IF-state,\\ncomparison, or EX-to-IF update routing to lose more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to make the profitable\\nstatic predictor cheaper rather than adding more prediction state. Today\\n`if_stage` forms bra...\n  Line 32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-025-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large architectural wins:\\nDIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the one-deep store slot reduces\\nrandom dmem-stall exposure. Recent micro-opt attempts that reshaped hot\\ndatapaths, predictor target logic, or EX/MEM metadata mostly lost Fmax to\\nplacement noise or new muxing. The next micro-opt should therefore target a\\nguaranteed always-present timing structure without changing IPC.\\n\\nThe top-level RVFI order counter is currently a single 64-bit increment:\\n`rvfi_order_q <= rvfi_order_q + 64'b1` on every retirement. The FPGA\\nbenchmark wrapper intentionally retains RVFI fanout by XOR-reducing the RVFI\\noutputs into an LED, so this counter cannot be assumed dead in synthesis.\\nA 64-bit carry self-loop is not architectural work for the CoreMark datapath,\\nbut it can still consume carry-chain/routing budget and compete with the hot\\npipeline for median Fmax.\\n\", ...\n  Line 33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-026-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Narrow Stall-Only I-Fetch Replay Cache\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction side. The 256-entry stall-only replay cache cut CoreMark cycles\\nfrom 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, showing that random iStall bubbles are a large\\npart of the remaining IPC loss, but its wide IF table/tag/data mux dropped\\nmedian Fmax enough to miss the current best.\\n\\nThat result points to a narrower replay structure rather than a broader\\nfetch queue. CoreMark's timed hot code repeatedly executes compact inner\\nloops and call bodies, so a smaller direct-mapped replay table should retain\\nmuch of the iStall hit-rate benefit while reducing the IF storage mux,\\ncomparator fanout, and PC-control load that hurt Fmax in the 256-entry\\nversion. This keeps the zero-wait path direct and targets only cycles that\\nwould otherwise be external imem stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the two structural/timing wins that have\\nactually paid off: cold DIV/REM is off the one-cycle EX path, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot hides store-only dmem stalls without hurting the\\nready-store path. The remaining measured structural opportunity is on the\\ninstruction