tors\\nlost more Fmax than their cycle savings recovered. The one exception with a\\nlarge cycle signal was the stall-only instruction replay experiment: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles substantially by serving hot-loop instructions during random\\nimem stalls, but its 256-entry full-tag table pulled median Fmax below the\\ncurrent best.\\n\\nThat result suggests a narrower predictor, not a new branch-direction table.\\nCoreMark's timed region repeatedly executes a relatively small hot text\\nfootprint, so a smaller replay predictor should reta...\n  Line 35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-028-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Byte-Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the RVFI order counter in `rtl/core.sv` from four 16-bit chunks t...\n  Line 36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\\narithmetic rewrite.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor pat...\n  Line 37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-030-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay tables kept much of the same\\ntiming problem while giving up hit rate.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another direction table\\nor return predictor, but a timing-clean replay predictor: keep the large\\niStall-hiding benefit, while moving the table lookup off the same-cycle IF\\ndecision path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state 