w cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That means an external imem stall served by\\nreplay still has a flop-to-flop path from `replay_cand_instr_q` through\\nbranch/JAL immediate reconstruction, target adders, the next-PC mux, and the\\nPC register. The replay table already knows the instruction for that PC from\\nan earlier live fetch, so its static next-PC prediction can be cached at fill\\ntime and replayed as metadata instead of recomputed in the hot stall path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It keeps the large random-iStall cycle\\nwin of the 256-entry replay cache while avoiding a same-cycle table lookup\\non the IF decision path. Later predictor experiments show that adding new\\nbranch-direction tables, return predictors, victim entries, or wider replay\\nlines either saves very few cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That ...\n  Line 50: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-043-r17s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-Only Hot/Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opcode/mux shape, so this remains the\\ncleanest unevaluated micro-opt angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opc...\n  Line 51: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-044-r17s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MRU Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry most-recently-used normal-RAM load replay latch in MEM. Each\\nentry stores a valid bit, an aligned word address tag, and the raw 32-bit word\\nreturned...\n  Line 52: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-045-r17s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead Agree Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned direction-table lookup into a\\nregistered lookahead candidate for the next fetch PC. That preserves the\\ncurrent IF replay structure and avoids adding a current-cycle counter-array\\nread to the branch/JAL target and fetch-ready path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned d...\n  Line 53: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-046-r18s0\", \"title\": \"Source-Qualified Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has many pointer\\nupdates, immediate arithmetic, loads, and control instructions after loads,\\nso these false matches can still inject bubbles even after the instruction\\nreplay predictor improves frontend availability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has...\n  Line 54: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Exact Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX h...\n  Line 55: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-048-r18s2\", \"title\": \"Kill-Gated I-Fetch Replay Fills\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The current IF\\nstage refreshes the replay table on every live `io_imemReady` fetch, even\\nwhen that fetch is being killed by an EX redirect or IF flush. Those killed\\nwords are architecturally correct immutable instructions, but they are\\nwrong-path for the current execution and can evict useful hot-loop replay\\nentries in the direct-mapped table. Avoiding this pollution is a narrow\\nreplay-predictor improvement with little timing exposure: it changes the\\nreplay write-enable policy, not the current-cycle fetch-ready, branch/JAL\\ntarget, ALU, memory, or retirement paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The cu...\n  Line 56: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-049-r19s0\", \"title\": \"Bitfield RV32M Control Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from making the ALU opcode encoding expose a\\nhot RV32I path and a cold RV32M path. That change improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which suggests the next micro-opt should keep\\nexploiting the same encoding rather than adding another replay table,\\npredictor, queue, reset rewrite, or arithmetic unit.\\n\\nSome downstream control logic still decodes RV32M operations with explicit\\nequality trees. In particular, EX detects divide/remainder instructions with\\nfour full `alu_op == ALU_*` compares even though the current encoding makes\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU exactly the `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` class. The divider\\nalso reclassifies signed versus unsigned and quotient versus remainder with\\nopcode equality checks even though those are now just low funct3 bits inside\\nthe divide group.\\n\\nThese compares are small, but they sit in always-present EX control and\\nlong-operation stall logic that feeds the frontend hold network. Recent\\nexperiments show broad datapath, reset, and replay changes often lose more\\nplacement quality than they recover, so a narrow decode cleanup in the\\nalready-proven ALU-op encoding is the most attractive micro_opt follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from making the ALU opcode encoding expose a\\nhot RV32I path and a cold RV32M path. That change improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which suggests the next micro-opt should keep\\nexploiting the same encoding rather than adding another replay table,\\npredictor, queue, reset rewrite, or arithmetic unit.\\n\\nSome downstream control logic still decodes RV32M operations with explicit\\nequality trees. In particular, EX detects divide/remainder instructions with\\nfour full `alu_op == ALU_*` compares even though the current encoding makes\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU exactly the `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` class. The divider...\n  Line 57: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-050-r19s1\", \"title\": \"Backend-Stall Shadow I-Fetch Prefetcher\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use ...\n  Line 58: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-051-r19s2\", \"title\": \"XOR-Indexed I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tables lost hit rate, and adding ways or\\nvictim entries added too much logic. A hashed direct-map index targets the\\nsame conflict misses while keeping the table size, registered lookahead\\ntiming model, live-imem-first policy, and compressed tag storage intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tabl...\n  Line 59: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-052-r20s0\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Aligned Hot ALU Encoding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should shrink\\ndecode and mux logic on the single-cycle EX result path while preserving the\\nsuccessful `op[4]` M-extension discriminator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should...\n  Line 60: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-053-r20s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Word Store Slot Coalescing\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a pending normal-RAM store is already occupying\\nthe slot and dmem is not ready, a younger store to the same aligned word does\\nnot need a second physical buffer entry. It can be merged into the pending\\nslot because loads and MMIO are still held behind the slot, and there is no\\narchitectural observer between the two normal-RAM stores. This targets the\\nsame store-burst pressure as the failed two-entry FIFO, but with one address\\ncompare and one byte-mask merge instead of another queue entry or a wider\\ndmem arbiter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already includes the structural wins that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot for normal-RAM store stalls\\nand a registered instruction replay table for random iStall. Broader store\\nbuffering attempts found the right bottleneck but were too expensive:\\ntwo-entry pending-store designs either failed placement, and the same-cycle\\nrefill variant also failed placement. That suggests the next structural\\nstore-side step should add capacity only for cases the existing single slot\\ncan handle locally.\\n\\nCoreMark updates list nodes, matrix cells, stack frames, and scalar state with\\nshort bursts of stores. When a ...\n  Line 61: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-054-r20s2\", \"title\": \"Banked Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best remaining replay opportunity is to reduce direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses without adding an associative lookup or changing the current-cycle\\nfetch-ready path. A full victim buffer added several tag compares and\\nregressed. XOR indexing changed conflicts but did not improve the measured\\nhit-rate/Fmax product. A banked direct-mapped predictor is a narrower\\nalternative: keep two small direct-mapped replay banks and choose between\\nthem with a simple per-set MRU bit during registered lookahead generation.\\nThe current cycle still consumes only the already registered replay\\ncandidate, so any extra bank selection work stays off the IF replay-hit path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the 256-entry registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table with compressed resetless tags. It is the only\\npredictor-family change with a large measured cycle signal, because it turns\\nrandom imem stalls into useful hot-instruction replays while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts that added branch-direction state, return targets,\\nvictim entries, wider lines, cached next-PC metadata, or XOR indexing either\\nsaved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe best...\n  Line 62: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-055-r21s0\", \"title\": \"Bank-Aware Replay Fill Select\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses more Fmax\\nthan it recovers, so the next micro-opt should keep the successful two-bank\\norganization and trim redundant logic inside it.\\n\\nThe current banked replay table still does two same-cycle tag comparisons for\\nevery live imem fill:\\n`replay_fill_bank0_match` and `replay_fill_bank1_match` re-read the current\\nPC's indexed set to decide whether to refresh bank 0, refresh bank 1, or\\nreplace the non-MRU bank. But the registered lookahead candidate for the\\ncurrent fetch PC was produced by comparing exactly those two banks on the\\nprevious cycle. If that candidate hits and its PC guard matches the current\\nPC, it already identifies that the word is present and which bank supplied\\nit. If it misses, the previous lookup already proved that neither bank held\\nthe current tag, so replacement can use the non-MRU bank directly.\\n\\nReusing the registered candidate's bank for live fills should remove two\\ncurrent-PC tag compare trees and part of the fill-bank muxing from the IF\\nreplay block while keeping the current replay hit rate, table size, MRU\\npolicy, candidate PC guard, and live-imem-first behavior intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the banked registered instruction\\nreplay predictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is now\\nboth the largest remaining IPC win and one of the most timing-sensitive IF\\nstructures. Follow-up replay experiments show that changing capacity,\\nindexing, victim search, or cached next-PC metadata usually loses ...\n  Line 63: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-056-r21s1\", \"title\": \"Pending Store Slot Load Forwarding\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currently blocks every younger memory\\noperation until the physical write drains, even if the younger operation is a\\nload of bytes that are already fully present in the pending store entry. That\\ncase does not require a second store-buffer entry or a dmem access: in\\nprogram order the store is already older and retired, so a same-word covered\\nload should read the store slot's bytes. CoreMark's stack, list, and state\\ncode can produce store/load reuse on nearby words, especially under random\\ndmem stalls that keep the slot valid for multiple cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural pieces that have survived\\nFPGA evaluation: a one-deep pending-store slot hides normal-RAM store\\nbackpressure, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random iStall bubbles. The structural experiments since then show the\\nboundary clearly. Larger store buffers and same-cycle refill failed\\nplacement, same-word store coalescing did not reduce CoreMark cycles, and\\ndata replay tables recovered some load-stall cycles but cost too much Fmax.\\n\\nThe remaining store-slot bottleneck is narrower. When a normal-RAM store has\\nretired into the pending slot, the core currentl...\n  Line 64: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-057-r21s2\", \"title\": \"Skewed-Bank I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family structure that has delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank targets the same conflict class with no extra\\ntable entries, no victim search, and no current-cycle table read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family structure that has delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank...\n  Line 65: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-058-r22s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay MRU State\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\