 table, which reached 577.76 fitness by hiding random imem stalls\\nwithout putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the remaining boundary clearly: adding more branch\\ndirection state, return prediction, victim search, skewed indexing, cached\\nnext-PC metadata, or bank-aware fill logic either saved too few cycles or\\ncost more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe least intrusive remaining replay opportunity is replacement policy. The\\ncurrent two-bank table uses a per-index MRU bit and marks every miss-filled\\nentry as MRU. That is a normal two-way cache policy, but it is vulnerable to\\none-shot or low-reuse...\n  Line 68: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-061-r23s0\", \"title\": \"Power-of-Two RAM Range Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned 32-bit\\ncomparison against `32'h0010_0000`. That limit is exactly 1 MiB, so for a\\n32-bit byte address the predicate is equivalent to `addr[31:20] == 0`.\\nThe result feeds store-slot enqueue and MEM backpressure, which in turn\\ndrives the global stall network. Replacing the generic less-than form with\\nan explicit high-bit decode should remove carry/comparator structure from\\nthe store-bufferable path without changing the direct ready-store behavior,\\npending-store semantics, memory ordering, or C