lus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patter...\n  Line 73: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-066-r24s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead JALR Target Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family mechanism that has produced a\\nlarge CoreMark cycle win without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. Follow-up replay changes, direction tables, return stacks,\\nvictim buffers, skewed indexes, and cached static next-PC metadata mostly\\nlost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining control-flow hole is narrower: JALR still resolves only in EX.\\nEvery return and indirect call therefore pays the wrong-path recovery cost\\neven when the target is stable from one execution of that JALR PC to the\\nnext. Earlier return predictors showed that JALR bubbles exist, but they put\\ncurrent-cycle compares or stack bookkeeping directly in IF. The successful\\nreplay predictor suggests a better timing shape: look up any target state one\\ncycle ahead, register a candidate for the current fetch PC, and keep the\\ncurrent IF cycle dependent only on that candidate plus the fetched\\ninstruction's opcode bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family mechanism that has produced a\\nlarge CoreMark cycle win without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. Follow-up replay changes, direction tables, return stacks,\\nvictim buffers, skewed indexes, and cached static next-PC metadata mostly\\nlost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining control-flow hole is narrower: JALR still resolves only in EX.\\nEvery return and indirect call therefore pays the wrong-path recovery cost\\neven when the target is stable from one execution of that JALR PC to the\\nnext. Earlier return predictors showed that JALR bubbles exist, but they put\\ncurrent-cycle compares or stack bookkeeping directly in IF. ...\n  Line 74: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-068-r25s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Last-Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\