rain or replace the cache from resolved taken branches and JAL/JALR redirects through the existing redirect path.\"\n26:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n27:     description: \"Thread the resolved transfer metadata between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"\n\n(End of file - total 27 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\ntitle: \"Two-entry hot target cache\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark keeps revisiting the same loop back-edges and a small set of\n  call/return sites. The prior single-entry hot-target idea shows this class\n  of predictor can pay off, but one slot can thrash when two hot transfers\n  alternate. A second bounded entry could raise hit rate without turning IF\n  into a general BTB or adding branch-immediate decode to the fetch cone.\nhypothesis: |\n  Expand the hot-transfer predictor into a tiny two-entry cache keyed by the\n  fetch PC, with both entries trained only from resolved taken control-flow\n  transfers. Keep fetch-side lookup to two PC compares plus a mux, and keep\n  training on the existing EX redirect path so no branch-immediate decode or\n  large history structure enters IF. The extra slot should preserve the loop\n  back-edge while also remembering a hot call/return site, reducing recurring\n  wrong-path flushes in CoreMark without materially widening the predictor cone.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r6s2.yaml","time":{"start":1778871662615,"end":1778871662627}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_01b7bc248768aeb0016a076d6dd7d8819aab9ef99fd8c605e2"}},"id":"prt_e2d03759d0017n2a4rre5PZddA","sessionID":"ses_1d2fe2f76ffeLmDxOQPnceYbHn","messageID":"msg_e2d0369c8001zFWm5jy1WC2Ut2"}}
