{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778872833916,"sessionID":"ses_1d2ebb3fcffebdfmGiBJ3A0dFz","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_2Gu9IE360jFD22fGjF7fefZW","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r7s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\n+title: \"Two-entry return stack\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark and the runtime library spend real time in call/return traffic,\n+  but this core still resolves every JALR return after fetch. A bounded\n+  return predictor should cut those recurring wrong-path flushes without\n+  pulling branch-immediate decode or a general BTB into the IF critical path.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny two-entry return-address stack that predicts canonical link-\n+  register returns from the fetched opcode/rd/rs1 pattern, and train it only\n+  from resolved JAL/JALR control flow in EX. Keep B-type branches and non-\n+  return JALRs on the existing EX-resolved path, so the predictor stays small,\n+  formal-friendly, and focused on the call/return behavior CoreMark actually\n+  exercises.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a 2-entry return-address stack in fetch and use it to choose the next PC for canonical return-like JALR instructions.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Generate stack push/pop update information from resolved JAL and JALR control flow, and redirect on return mispredicts.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the predictor update signals between EX and IF without changing the RVFI or memory IO contract.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r7s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tourna