{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778875405121,"sessionID":"ses_1d2c52a4cffe4L1kjYjaQmo4b8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_DeA451AqdrspR9AODziK8wmI","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\n+title: \"Two-entry backedge cache\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark’s tight loops hit the same backward branches over and over, but the core still pays an EX-resolved flush on every first encounter. A predictor that caches only the hottest taken backedges can turn those repeats into same-cycle fetches without dragging a full BTB, return stack, or extra decode logic into IF.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny two-entry exact-match backedge cache in IF and train it only from resolved taken backward B-type branches. Use the cached target on a PC hit, ignore JAL/JALR and forward branches, and keep replacement to a simple bounded policy so the hardware stays small and the fetch cone stays short. This should recover repeated loop backedges with less logic than the earlier general-purpose predictor attempts.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Thread the resolved branch metadata from EX into IF so the cache can train on taken backedges.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Surface the resolved branch outcome and target metadata needed for predictor updates.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add the two-entry backedge cache lookup, update, and predicted-next-PC selection in fetch.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-003-r9s2.yaml\n======================